WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:17.710
Tim Ferriss: Dr. Attia, Peter, nice to see
you.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:19.340
Dr. Peter Attia: Good to see you, man.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.610
Tim Ferriss: I wanted to start with the personal,
we're going to get into all sorts of topics,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:31.390
many different aspects of your practice, lessons
learned, et cetera but I went to your house

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.390
recently.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.140
We had some Topo Chico and a bite to eat and
a few other things.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:41.620
And I commented on how much larger you were
and not larger as in obese, but muscularly

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.250
larger.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:49.710
So I thought we would begin with the question
of how and why, not necessarily in that order,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.930
you added so much muscle mass.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:01:04.040
Dr. Peter Attia: In the fall of 2021, I had
sort of hit a relative low in weight that

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:09.140
wasn't quite as low as where I was as a cyclist,
which was probably about 165 pounds, but was

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.189
still pretty low for me in the maybe mid-low
to mid 170s.

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:18.440
And I did a DEXA scan, which I do periodically,
probably a couple times a year, but I hadn't

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.960
done one in a few years.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.149
And when I get my DEXA scan, I always plot
the data.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:29.659
So I'm not just interested in body fat, but
also bone mineral content and many other metrics.

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:33.880
But one of them that's really important is
something called Appendicular Lean Mass Index

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:34.990
or ALMI.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.430
Another one is called FFMI, Fat-Free Mass
Index.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:45.640
And it's probably worth explaining a little
bit about what these are, but the ALMI takes

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:51.799
what the DEXA scan imputes as the lean tissue
in your arms and legs in kilograms divides

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:53.759
that by your height in meters squared.

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:57.600
So it spits out a number like eight kilograms
per meter squared.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.480
This is a pretty accurate representation of
how much muscle you have in your arms and

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:08.720
legs because the DEXA scan can really only
identify three things, bone fat and other.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.450
And since things in your arms that are not
bone and fat tend to be muscle, it's pretty

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.349
good at identifying muscle, whereas the Fat-Free
Mass Index looks at the entire body and just

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:18.690
subtracts out fat.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:22.959
That's also a good proxy for lean mass, but
there are many other things like organs that

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:24.569
are taking up some residents there.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:27.930
Anyway, when I looked at these metrics, I
was shocked.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:35.379
I was shocked at how much less muscle I had
than 10 years earlier, almost exactly 10 years

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:36.379
sooner.

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:40.290
And I attribute it to several things, but
I really thought that chief among them was

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:45.099
just how much fasting I had been doing over
the previous three years.

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:51.709
I had really kind of taken fasting to a pretty
extreme place doing up to 10 days of water

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:57.989
only, and routinely just busting out seven-day
water-only fasts, certainly doing three days

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:01.330
every month would be something I would do
without hesitation.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:05.680
Tim Ferriss: When you say 10 days of water
only, over what period of time is that?

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.640
Dr. Peter Attia: Meaning how frequently would
I do that?

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:11.840
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm just wondering what
the cadence was if we take it as a minimal

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.220
—
Dr. Peter Attia: Probably quarterly.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.220
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.829
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, probably every quarter.

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:20.459
And while I certainly think there are some
benefits from doing that, I think what I was

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:26.520
sort of confronting was, wow, there's actually
a pretty big cost as well.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.209
And I would go out of my way while fasting
to still exercise.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:35.620
I was lifting weights every single day, which
was difficult sometimes, but doing everything

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.430
I could to try to stimulate muscle protein
synthesis.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:42.620
But at the end of the day, if you're not providing
any nutrients, including amino acids, you're

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.500
basically just tearing down muscle to try
to rebuild with that muscle.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.420
But your net effect is a loss.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.909
So I kind of just said, "I've got to fix this.

00:03:50.909 --> 00:03:57.650
I've got to do something about this, and I
want to have my ALMI be in at least the 90th

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:01.689
percentile, if not above the 97th percentile
as I age."

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:08.010
The data are pretty unambiguous that people
live longer, better lives with an ALMI north

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.490
of the 75th percentile.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:16.570
So not one to just clear the bar, I want to
be considerably above the bar.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:20.630
So basically I just changed things around
and said, "Nutrition and exercise are going

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.810
to change a little bit," so we can talk about
those changes, but basically I've made big

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.210
changes with exercise and nutrition to make
those corrections.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:34.020
Tim Ferriss: How much lean mass did you gain
over what period of time would you say once

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.120
making these changes?

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.460
Dr. Peter Attia: So in a span of, oh, God,
I'd have to go back and look at the exact

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:45.610
data, I believe it was 13 or 14 pounds of
lean mass in about 12 months.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:46.610
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.850
And what were —
Dr. Peter Attia: Which is, again, that's not

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:50.020
Herculean, right?

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:58.240
These are not insane numbers, not taking anabolic
steroids, this is relatively doable for someone

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:05.130
who is making it a high priority to eat the
right amount of protein, the right kind of

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:11.210
protein, and spacing it and consuming it at
the right times, and then also focusing, training

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.550
on a little more hypertrophy.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.840
Tim Ferriss: So for people listening, I'm
asking these questions not simply because

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.039
I'm interested in lean mass, but also the
implications for longevity.

00:05:21.039 --> 00:05:26.600
So I'm guessing you did not put all of that
lean muscle on your biceps and that there

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:34.139
were other movements that were incorporated
for very particular reasons if I'm getting

00:05:34.139 --> 00:05:38.530
my read on Dr. Peter Attia, right as I think
I am.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:44.889
So perhaps you could just describe some of
those changes and also protein and how you

00:05:44.889 --> 00:05:48.690
suggest people think about protein, how you
began thinking about protein.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.990
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, I'll start with that
and then we can talk about the training.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:59.009
So the protein basically went to a very conscious
and concerted effort to increase protein to

00:05:59.009 --> 00:06:01.400
one gram per pound of body weight.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.760
People are going to ask, "Is that one pound
of actual body weight?

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.300
Is that one pound of lean body weight?

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.990
Is that one pound of target body weight?"

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:14.169
The answer is for someone my size and weight
and my body fat percentage, I think at the

00:06:14.169 --> 00:06:17.680
time I was about 15 percent body fat, it really
doesn't matter.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.970
It could be basically, call it one pound of
current body weight, one gram per pound of

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.120
current body weight is sufficient.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.660
So that's what I did.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.449
The next question that becomes timing.

00:06:27.449 --> 00:06:31.979
And this is something that right until a couple
years ago, I wasn't really paying enough attention

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:32.979
to.

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:37.620
So it wasn't enough to just say, "Well, Peter,
you've got to have 180 grams of protein in

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:44.940
a day," because for example, 290 gram bolus
of protein doesn't accomplish nearly the same

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:50.550
thing as 445 gram servings spread out over
the course of a day.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:55.880
So there is kind of a sweet spot with protein
where you really need to be north of about

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:01.070
20 to 25 grams to ensure that those amino
acids aren't just going into what's called

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:02.199
a gluconeogenic pathway.

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:07.910
So we can turn protein into glucose, but we
don't really want to, protein should be thought

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:12.150
of as a structural nutrient, whereas we want
to really think of carbohydrates and fats

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.840
as energy nutrients.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:19.280
So you have to have enough protein in a given
serving that you don't just have the liver

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.370
take it and use its bit for gluconeogenesis.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:32.900
At the same time you don't want to have too
much and too much is a bit of an unclear limit,

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:38.750
but my best reading of the literature is that
you're starting to hit too much at about 50

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:39.750
grams in a serving.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:44.129
Tim Ferriss: And what type of protein are
we talking about just in terms of, I'm thinking

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:47.360
of bioavailability and so on, and kind of
protein efficiency ratio.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.729
What type of protein, how should people think
about quality or type of protein?

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:54.020
Dr. Peter Attia: So the first decision I think
everybody wants to make is, am I getting this

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:56.120
from plant or animal?

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:01.250
And again, unfortunately, this has become
sort of a contentious topic and it's got its

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.159
sort of political religious overtones to it.

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:07.670
But if you don't concern yourself with any
of those things and you just think about biochemistry,

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.390
there's really no ambiguity here.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.170
Protein derived from animal sources are more
bioavailable.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:15.210
So you can overcome some of that by cooking
the plant.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:20.210
So once you start to cook the plant and free
it up from the fiber, you can liberate more

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:25.629
of the plant protein, but you're still always
going to be struggling with a bit of a bioavailability

00:08:25.629 --> 00:08:26.629
problem.

00:08:26.629 --> 00:08:30.210
That can be anywhere from a 20 percent to
30 percent haircut, you're taking just on

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.010
the net consumption of the amino acid.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:36.229
The second issue comes down to amino acid
quality.

00:08:36.229 --> 00:08:42.080
And if you're really concerning yourself with
hypertrophy, I think the three most important

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.600
amino acids would be leucine, lysine, and
methionine.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:56.329
And these are amino acids that also you don't
tend to have as high a quantity in plant protein.

00:08:56.329 --> 00:09:02.940
Now you can get them, and it gets especially
hard if you can't have dairy or eggs.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.360
So if someone says, "Look, I'm a vegetarian,
I'm not going to eat beef or chicken, but

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.940
I'm willing to have eggs and dairy," it gets
a lot easier.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:13.750
Eggs, for example, are a very high source
of methionine.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:21.700
So what we typically will tell patients is
rather than just fixating on the total number

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:27.110
of grams of protein you need to eat, which
you may have a hard time hitting, if you focus

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:31.190
on the number of grams of leucine, lycine,
and methionine you're getting in a given meal

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:36.020
or a given day, that will almost assuredly
get your total protein quantity to the right

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:37.020
place.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:42.930
So for me, that really meant, I just wanted
to make sure I was getting, gosh, probably

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:49.060
six to eight grams of leucine a day, comparable
amount of lysine, at least two grams of methionine

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:50.420
a day.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:56.680
And I use an app to track what I'm eating
just so that I can see where my protein is

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:01.060
coming from and how much I'm getting and when
and where and that tended to work out pretty

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:02.060
well.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.870
Tim Ferriss: What is the name of the app if
you're willing to mention it?

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.940
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, it's called Carbon.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:12.070
I have no affiliation with it, but a friend
of mine, it's his app and I like it.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.350
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:17.690
Dr. Peter Attia: I guess my affiliation is
that it's my friend's app.

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:23.290
Tim Ferriss: I'm glad you brought up the leucine,
lysine, and methionine, because I was going

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:30.990
to ask earlier if while you were fasting,
but losing lean muscle mass over time, you

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.519
were supplementing with say, branch chain
amino acids in any fashion.

00:10:34.519 --> 00:10:35.830
Dr. Peter Attia: I was.

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:41.000
I used to always drink BCAAs during workouts
and for folks listening, there were three

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.889
branch chain amino acids.

00:10:43.889 --> 00:10:50.310
And leucine is hands down the most relevant
of them and we know from work that our mutual

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:57.910
friend David Sabatini has done in his lab
that leucine is a very, very potent mTOR stimulator.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:04.269
In fact, it might be the single most potent
stimulator of mTOR, mTOR, of course, acutely,

00:11:04.269 --> 00:11:05.680
you want that thing stimulated.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:06.820
It's promoting growth.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:10.050
You don't want it chronically stimulated,
but you want it acutely stimulated.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:13.750
The problem with branch chain amino acids,
and I've got to tell you, Tim, I think it's

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:15.340
largely fallen out of favor.

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:17.540
So I don't even consume branch chain amino
acids anymore.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:21.339
When I'm during a workout, I'm literally just
consuming like an electrolyte drink.

00:11:21.339 --> 00:11:22.339
That's it.

00:11:22.339 --> 00:11:28.579
The problem is, one, they don't stick around
long enough and they're free form, and two,

00:11:28.579 --> 00:11:30.839
you're just not getting enough of them.

00:11:30.839 --> 00:11:36.389
The third thing is the temporal nature of
when you take them in being in the workout

00:11:36.389 --> 00:11:40.690
or couple hours after the workout doesn't
really seem to matter, our window in which

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:47.100
we can synthesize new muscle is sufficiently
broad that even for an untrained individual

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.510
who has a narrower window, it's totally reasonable
to say, look, just make sure a couple hours

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:54.829
after that workout, you get in your big protein
meal.

00:11:54.829 --> 00:12:00.459
So instead, today I just use high-quality
whey protein post-workout, and I don't trouble

00:12:00.459 --> 00:12:02.950
myself with consuming any protein in workout.

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:06.139
Tim Ferriss: So I just want to mention a few
things for folks.

00:12:06.139 --> 00:12:14.690
So number one, there's a huge difference between
no animal products and lacto-ovo vegetarian.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:19.750
And one example of that would be Bill Pearl,
who became Mr. Universe as a lacto-ovo vegetarian.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:21.240
So it does make a huge difference.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.300
The second I'll just mention, we may come
back to this and discuss keto at some point,

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:31.899
we have discussed it at length in previous
conversations, but the gluconeogenesis is

00:12:31.899 --> 00:12:37.149
one of, well, is I suppose the pathway by
which you can knock yourself out of keto if

00:12:37.149 --> 00:12:42.020
you consume too much protein and not enough
fat.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:46.540
Liver just loves to convert that into glucose
and then bada bing, bada boom, you're out

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:51.140
of ketosis, or at least out of sort of the
range that one would be aiming for.

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:53.699
So let's come to the book.

00:12:53.699 --> 00:12:57.410
So you and I have had many offline conversations
about the book.

00:12:57.410 --> 00:12:59.160
We'll probably not repeat some of them.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.589
Why did it take so long?

00:13:01.589 --> 00:13:08.410
And I believe there were three iterations
over six years, and the first iteration had

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.840
a zero translation into the final version.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:17.680
Something along those lines, maybe you could
correct me if I'm getting that wrong, but

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.420
why have you taken so much time to work on
this book?

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:22.410
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, it's a really good
question, Tim.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:26.100
I think there are probably several factors,
some of which are related, some of which are

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:27.579
not.

00:13:27.579 --> 00:13:37.120
I think on one level, my appetite for how
"good" I needed this book to be was very high.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:38.390
And I say that with no —
Tim Ferriss: So unlike like you, Peter.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:40.120
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:44.209
Look, I think there are some people out there
who can churn out a book a year, and more

00:13:44.209 --> 00:13:46.209
power to them.

00:13:46.209 --> 00:13:50.560
But the way I viewed this book was that this
is sort of the one and only book I ever want

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:51.560
to write.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.630
Will I ever write another book?

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:58.459
It's hard for me to ever imagine the answer
to that question is yes, but again, maybe

00:13:58.459 --> 00:14:01.380
at the end of my life I'll have something
else to say.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.310
As I'm 50 years old now and I'm at the midpoint
of my life or slightly past the midpoint of

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:09.810
my life, I feel like everything I ever want
to say on this particular topic is in this

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:10.820
book.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:14.990
And therefore, it was just a very long book.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:19.170
I mean, the final version is probably 140,000
words.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:22.880
The version I ultimately submitted was 200,000
words.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:30.459
This has been chopped way down by 30 percent
into what is still obviously a book that is

00:14:30.459 --> 00:14:31.889
quite long.

00:14:31.889 --> 00:14:33.329
So that's part of it.

00:14:33.329 --> 00:14:39.540
I also think part of it is, and maybe you
can relate to this, Tim, I don't know, we've

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:43.889
never actually talked about this, but I think
there's just a vulnerability that comes with

00:14:43.889 --> 00:14:47.269
writing in this manner that is unlike all
the writing I've done in the past.

00:14:47.269 --> 00:14:55.250
So I've been blogging insanely for 12 years,
that's different.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:59.980
You're not really putting yourself out there
when you write a blog post.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.149
You don't have to read the comments that people
write.

00:15:02.149 --> 00:15:04.500
You don't have to pay attention to any of
that stuff.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:11.800
I think when you write a book, you are really
saying to people like, "Here I am, tear me

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.060
down."

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:17.269
And I just think there's, on some level, maybe
it's embarrassing to say this, there's just

00:15:17.269 --> 00:15:24.090
been a little bit of a reluctance to be that
again, for lack of a better word, vulnerable,

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:27.810
just to sort of say, "Okay, I'm willing to
say everything I have to say on a subject

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.690
matter and now let the arrows come flying."

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:33.581
And that's, again, I don't know that that
makes sense, but I'm sure that anyone who

00:15:33.581 --> 00:15:36.620
does something very public feels that way.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:39.630
But it's different with podcasting, which
I don't have a problem doing, and it's different

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:42.250
with blogging, which I don't have a problem
doing.

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:46.960
But this strikes me as maybe what an actor
feels like when they make a big film or a

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:49.079
director, or again, like an author like you.

00:15:49.079 --> 00:15:56.149
So I think there was a little bit of subconscious
dragging my feet and hey, maybe I can just

00:15:56.149 --> 00:15:59.550
keep working on this book forever, but it'll
never actually come out.

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:05.759
Tim Ferriss: So I do identify, and I would
say for me at least, part of that is when

00:16:05.759 --> 00:16:11.139
you put out a book, it might be one of the
