00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:41.750
you.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:42.750
That means a lot.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:43.750
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, definitely.

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:50.380
And with that, let's talk about perhaps this
is a good place to start, objective, strategy,

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:51.380
tactics.

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:56.270
Why are these three pillars important, and
why all three?

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:59.870
Dr. Peter Attia: I learned about this stuff
about a decade ago when I was working on a

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:06.240
problem that is irrelevant now, but I think
the message was important, which was, I think

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:12.419
I had a vague sense of what the objective
was, and my immediate tendency, which I think

00:18:12.419 --> 00:18:17.040
is normal, and I think we all do, because
I don't think anyone's hardwired to go through

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:19.780
the middle step, was to go from objective
to tactics.

00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:24.000
In fact, I think most people just kind of
go to tactics without even thinking about

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.620
objective.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:31.809
And certainly not having that strategy piece
in the middle makes life really complicated.

00:18:31.809 --> 00:18:34.460
So the first thing, I think, is to understand
what's the difference.

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:37.260
So objective is pretty straightforward.

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:38.630
It needs to be stated.

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:42.679
It should be very clear, though.

00:18:42.679 --> 00:18:51.809
So for example, my objective might be to live
longer.

00:18:51.809 --> 00:18:54.059
If you said, "Well, what are the tactics now?

00:18:54.059 --> 00:18:55.059
What should I eat?

00:18:55.059 --> 00:18:56.059
How should I exercise?

00:18:56.059 --> 00:18:58.300
Do I need to take this supplement or that
supplement?"

00:18:58.300 --> 00:19:05.080
The probability that you are going to come
up with the right set of instructions is vanishingly

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:06.080
small.

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:07.080
It's somewhere between zero.

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:11.679
Tim Ferriss: Even if provided this list, this
laundry list of tactics.

00:19:11.679 --> 00:19:15.000
Dr. Peter Attia: Well, I guess the question
is how do you come up with the list?

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.070
Are you filtering on that list?

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:18.070
Right?

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:21.710
So there's no shortage of tactics that one
could think about because the world is rife

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:23.720
with everybody offering their tactics.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:27.909
But it's not like I'm not suggesting tactics,
but what I really want people to do is say,

00:19:27.909 --> 00:19:29.640
"Look, let's anchor to a strategy."

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:34.071
So for example, if one of your objectives
is, "Okay, I'm going to live longer," it's

00:19:34.071 --> 00:19:35.480
like, no, let's deconstruct that.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.059
What does living longer mean?

00:19:37.059 --> 00:19:41.400
Well, to understand what it means to live
longer, you have to understand what ends life.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:48.570
Well, what ends life is a very clear and easy-to-understand
dataset so let's look at it and let's look

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:49.620
at it in great detail.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:52.100
We call this the death bars analysis.

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:53.180
So what are the death bars?

00:19:53.180 --> 00:20:00.360
Well, it turns out if you're over 40, you
don't smoke, we can really clearly tell you

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:05.669
that there's about an 80 percent chance you're
going to die from atherosclerotic cardiovascular

00:20:05.669 --> 00:20:11.770
disease, cancer, neurodegenerative disease,
or metabolic diseases such as type two diabetes

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.659
and things like that.

00:20:13.659 --> 00:20:18.990
Probably I would include accidental death
in there, so falling or automotive accidents.

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:22.549
So that's an important insight because now
you start to work backwards from there.

00:20:22.549 --> 00:20:23.549
So wait a minute.

00:20:23.549 --> 00:20:25.740
You think, oh, if I want to live longer, I
have to avoid those things.

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:28.039
Okay, how do I avoid those things?

00:20:28.039 --> 00:20:30.630
Well, let's start with the chronic disease
bucket.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:33.890
There's two ways to think about that, right?

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:39.419
I love when things turn into somewhat binary
decisions, is one way to live longer, to live

00:20:39.419 --> 00:20:43.820
longer with a chronic disease or live longer
without a chronic disease.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:49.320
So might not be entirely obvious which of
those is true, but if we look at the data,

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:51.140
it's pretty clear.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:56.610
The data unambiguously make it clear that
you will live longer if you can extend the

00:20:56.610 --> 00:21:01.529
period of time that you live without a chronic
disease, rather than extend the period of

00:21:01.529 --> 00:21:03.350
time you have the chronic disease.

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:08.570
So in other words, it's better to get cancer
later in life than to live longer with cancer

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:09.630
as one example.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:13.830
It's better to have your first heart attack
very late in life than have it early in life,

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:17.650
and then be right on the verge of having another
one as time goes on.

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:22.200
Well, just by going through those very simple
questions, all of a sudden you start to formulate

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:28.570
a much clearer strategy for living longer
that points you much more clearly towards

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:33.039
this idea I talked about in the book called
Medicine 3.0 rather than Medicine 2.0.

00:21:33.039 --> 00:21:36.730
And once you iron all that out, then you start
to get into the tactics.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:37.730
Oh, all right.

00:21:37.730 --> 00:21:38.730
Okay.

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:39.770
So how does nutrition layer onto this?

00:21:39.770 --> 00:21:41.510
How does pharmacology layer onto this?

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:44.940
How does exercise layer onto this, et cetera.

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:51.480
Tim Ferriss: I want to just share an anecdote,
which will not surprise you, but when I published

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:55.100
The 4-Hour Body, and no doubt you're going
to get a lot of these types of requests if

00:21:55.100 --> 00:22:03.130
you haven't already, I had a number of, not
morbidly obese, but out-of-shape CEO friends

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:06.130
say, "Look, I just want the index card.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.880
Just give me the index card with the seven
things I should do, and I'll do those seven

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:09.880
things."

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:13.110
Can you guess what the compliance rate was
with that, Peter?

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:14.150
What's your guess?

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:19.350
Dr. Peter Attia: Very close to the success
rate of skipping the strategy step.

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:21.429
Tim Ferriss: Zero percent.

00:22:21.429 --> 00:22:25.409
Not a single one of them actually implemented
any of it.

00:22:25.409 --> 00:22:33.110
And just as a quick side note, which you might
entertaining, I had an amazing mega-successful

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:40.330
investor named Ed Thorpe on the podcast, if
not over 90, 90 right now, he's in excellent

00:22:40.330 --> 00:22:48.059
shape and he plans his travel around minimizing
time in automobiles.

00:22:48.059 --> 00:22:50.850
And he's sort of run through the numbers just
as an anecdote.

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:55.450
Dr. Peter Attia: Very underappreciated, very
underappreciated cause of morbidity and mortality.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:57.539
Yeah, I think about it a lot.

00:22:57.539 --> 00:22:58.539
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:22:58.539 --> 00:23:03.851
We might come back to as I think about a different
seat belts that you can use in life, which

00:23:03.851 --> 00:23:08.900
initially, I don't want to turn this into
a Tim Ferriss TED Talk, but initially thought

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:16.860
about with respect to COVID early, which was
like what minimally inconvenient, easy-to-do

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:20.280
things can you do to dramatically mitigate
risk.

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:24.580
So even though you've never had a head-on
collision, the downside of having a head-on

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:29.330
collision is so high that most of us will
wear a seatbelt when driving on the highway.

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:33.309
Most of us will have a fire extinguisher in
the kitchen even though there's never been

00:23:33.309 --> 00:23:34.929
a fire in your kitchen, et cetera.

00:23:34.929 --> 00:23:43.080
So where else can we take very small steps
that are easy, low cost, that prevent dramatic

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:44.080
downside risk?

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:48.370
But so we may come back to that, but I want
to bring up a graph that you have in your

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:55.680
book, page 28 for those who want to find it,
where if you remove the top eight contagious/infectious

00:23:55.680 --> 00:24:04.570
diseases, meaning we're accounting for the
advent of antibiotics, at least as I understand

00:24:04.570 --> 00:24:05.570
it.

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:11.710
We haven't really seen a material or at least
large change in mortality rates since what,

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:15.240
1900 if you sort of account for some of these
variables.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.040
Why do you think that is?

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.350
Because certainly most people don't want to
die.

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:20.690
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.039
So you're absolutely right.

00:24:22.039 --> 00:24:27.559
So life expectancy since 1900 to today, has
increased by twofold.

00:24:27.559 --> 00:24:28.559
It's remarkable.

00:24:28.559 --> 00:24:32.610
It's gone from about 40 to about 80 plus or
minus, but that's directionally the change

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:36.180
we've seen in, call it 120 years.

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:43.850
And I do think that understandably, it's tempting
to tout that as the marvels of modern medicine.

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:51.410
And you're thinking, well, oh, my God, we
went from 40 to 80 in 120 years, the next

00:24:51.410 --> 00:24:56.400
40 just based on Moore's Law, that's got to
get us to like 160, right?

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:58.020
Okay, maybe.

00:24:58.020 --> 00:25:00.440
But let's take a closer look at the data.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:06.400
So you do this very simple analysis, which
is, okay, let's go back to 1900 and march

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:07.620
forward.

00:25:07.620 --> 00:25:13.630
But when we go through the mortality tables,
just strip out the top eight leading infectious

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:16.200
disease causes of death.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:19.030
And what you see is exactly what you describe.

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:24.490
There is now no change in mortality from then
until now effectively.

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:26.289
There's a little bit, it's been a little bit
better.

00:25:26.289 --> 00:25:32.140
And I think what it speaks to is two things.

00:25:32.140 --> 00:25:41.910
One is the remarkable success of Medicine
2.0 in solving acute care and infectious diseases,

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:49.390
and the remarkable failure of Medicine 2.0
in addressing the chronic diseases that sat

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:54.299
under the surface of the water waiting for
the tide to go down.

00:25:54.299 --> 00:26:02.490
Tim Ferriss: And those would be presumably
the four or five likely causes that you mentioned

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:03.490
earlier.

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:07.230
Perhaps this is a good place for you just
to define or describe briefly, not Web 2.0,

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:08.770
try that again, for you to describe medicine
— I'm not going to be the only interviewer

00:26:08.770 --> 00:26:10.730
who fucks that up, Medicine 2.0 and Medicine
3.0.

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:14.100
Dr. Peter Attia: It's probably easiest to
just for the take completeness, start with

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:16.480
Medicine 1.0.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:24.200
Medicine 1.0 was basically everything that
occurred prior to the transition period of

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:31.450
the late 17th century into the late 19th century.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:37.250
So that 200-year period marks a transition
from when medicine had absolutely no basis

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:38.250
in science whatsoever.

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:45.270
So I mean, we can mock it now, but truthfully,
they didn't have a scientific method or a

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:49.490
scientific tool, so all they could do was
sort of think about the gods, think about

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:55.549
bad humours come up with the best rationalization
they had for what was going on.

00:26:55.549 --> 00:27:03.149
In fact, arguably the most insightful thought
that occurred in Medicine 1.0 was thousands

00:27:03.149 --> 00:27:08.040
of years ago on the part of Hippocrates, who
was the first to believe that diseases were

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:10.740
somehow caused by nature and not the gods.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:14.820
That was truly a remarkable insight now, beyond
that, he had no clue what was going on, but

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:19.620
that was a remarkable insight, and that was
probably the apex of Medicine 1.0.

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:25.049
With Francis Bacon in the 17th century, we
had basically the first push towards the scientific

00:27:25.049 --> 00:27:26.500
method.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:31.660
This became really important, and no one has
done a better job explaining this than our

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:33.289
mutual hero, Richard Feynman.

00:27:33.289 --> 00:27:37.059
There's a great video, which I'm sure you
guys can find for the show notes, where it's

00:27:37.059 --> 00:27:41.159
an old grainy black and white where Feynman
is talking about the scientific method.

00:27:41.159 --> 00:27:46.070
And he's saying, "Look, you make a guess,
right?

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.480
You design an experiment.

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:51.690
The guess is called a hypothesis, by the way,
it's just a fancy word for a guess.

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:55.180
You design an experiment to test that hypothesis.

00:27:55.180 --> 00:27:56.740
You do the experiment.

00:27:56.740 --> 00:28:00.210
You compare the results of the experiment
to your guess.

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:05.279
And then you decide to either discard or update
your hypothesis."

00:28:05.279 --> 00:28:12.919
This was such a profound concept that it basically
propelled us into Medicine 2.0.

00:28:12.919 --> 00:28:20.360
And the place where that shone greatest was
unquestionably in the world of microbacteria,

00:28:20.360 --> 00:28:24.140
of microscopic organisms, so viruses and bacteria.

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:33.320
And so, again, it's a brutal, bloody slog
to go from Semmelweis to Koch to Lister.

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:39.169
But that transition effectively brought us
into the modern era where, hey, all of a sudden

00:28:39.169 --> 00:28:42.850
doctors were washing their hands and not operating
on people with dirty hands.

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:49.149
And of course, eventually we had antibiotics
and eventually we had vaccines.

00:28:49.149 --> 00:28:57.200
And taking smallpox and polio off the table
and reducing the mortality of influenza and

00:28:57.200 --> 00:29:01.970
having antibiotics, all of that stuff had
such a profound impact on human health that

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:06.049
it cut mortality rate down by a half and doubled
our life expectancy in the process.

00:29:06.049 --> 00:29:08.490
So now we're into the maturation of Medicine
2.0.

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:10.299
We saw these other amazing success stories.

00:29:10.299 --> 00:29:14.740
I think the two most notable by far would
be Hepatitis C and HIV.

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:22.860
I mean, to think that we're at a point now
where a virus that is as tricky as HIV can

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:28.549
basically be rendered a chronic disease on
the back of highly active antiretroviral therapy

00:29:28.549 --> 00:29:30.250
is nothing short of amazing.

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:33.510
When I was in medical school, Tim, we're not
talking a long time ago, this is 25 years

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:41.790
ago, we were told Hep C will never be cured
and it will break the back of the US and global

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:46.500
transplant infrastructure, meaning we're going
to need so many livers to put into all these

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:51.430
people with Hep C that, by 2030, the system
will break.

00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:56.659
And lo and behold, we have a drug that within
a matter of weeks will eradicate this thing.

00:29:56.659 --> 00:29:59.240
So again, huge successes.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:05.070
Now, counter that with what we've done against
cancer.

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:10.680
When Richard Nixon declared the War on Cancer,
before you and I were born, it was more than

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:17.810
50 years ago, the goal was to eradicate this
disease by the bicentennial in 1976.

00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:23.110
Well, not only did that not happen, but here
we are more than 50 years later, and survival

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:28.980
for cancer is barely five percent greater
than it was in 1970.

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:34.490
I could go on and on, but let's just look
at neurodegenerative disease.

00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:39.460
So you were talking about six million people
with Alzheimer's disease in this country.

00:30:39.460 --> 00:30:43.630
If you add up those with Lewy body dementia
and Parkinson's disease, we're talking about,

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:45.440
gosh, another four and a half million.

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:50.730
So we're looking at more than 10 million people
in this country with very, very significant

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:51.919
neurodegenerative diseases.

00:30:51.919 --> 00:30:56.360
And that says nothing about ALS and Huntington's
and things like that.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:31:03.760
We don't have a single thing, like a single
thing that we can do to cure these people.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:09.380
We have barely a few things that can slow
the progression of their diseases.

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:14.710
But we've made paltry steps against chronic
diseases.

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:20.690
When you and I were born, the prevalence of
type two diabetes was less than one percent.

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:24.250
Today it's 10 percent, effectively.

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:32.060
So we've seen a log fold increase in type
two diabetes. 100 to 120 million people in

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:38.649
the United States are metabolically sick,
meaning have metabolic syndrome or pre-diabetes,

00:31:38.649 --> 00:31:40.159
or type two diabetes outright.

00:31:40.159 --> 00:31:42.649
So something's not working.

00:31:42.649 --> 00:31:47.360
And the current system of Medicine 2.0 doesn't
really seem equipped to address it.

00:31:47.360 --> 00:31:51.610
It intervenes late, and by the time it's intervening,
it's not really fixing.

00:31:51.610 --> 00:31:55.500
So this thing I'm proposing, this idea that
of course I'm not the first person to suggest,

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:59.670
maybe I'm the first person to talk about it
this way, is we want to pivot to this newer

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:06.659
way of thinking about things which deviates
from Medicine 2.0 in a couple of really important

00:32:06.659 --> 00:32:08.580
areas.

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:11.179
The first is in its timeline.

00:32:11.179 --> 00:32:16.600
So Medicine 2.0 is largely focused on treating
disease.

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:23.480
And it's really interesting when I think about
it, because it's so obvious that I can't believe

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:24.950
I didn't notice it earlier.

00:32:24.950 --> 00:32:29.150
But when you're in medicine, when you're in
medical school, when you're in residency,

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:32.760
when you're in practice, you sort of need
a code for a disease.

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:37.510
You need a code for a disease that you bill
for and you treat.

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:40.299
And prevention doesn't really fit into that.

00:32:40.299 --> 00:32:45.750
So when I went to medical school, I didn't
learn a single thing about nutrition or exercise

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:48.519
or sleep or stress management or emotional
health.

00:32:48.519 --> 00:32:51.630
I learned a lot about pharmacology.

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:55.549
And it's tempting at this point to say, well,
pharmacology is either good or bad.

00:32:55.549 --> 00:32:58.050
No, it's both, right?

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:01.300
Pharmacology is good and it's really valuable,
but over-indexing it is bad.

00:33:01.300 --> 00:33:02.919
And ignoring all those other things is problematic.

00:33:02.919 --> 00:33:07.289
Those other things turned out to be far more
efficient tools of prevention.

00:33:07.289 --> 00:33:13.200
But if no doctor learned about them, then
why are we surprised that Medicine 2.0 is

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:14.789
unable to cope with that?

00:33:14.789 --> 00:33:19.010
So one, we have to understand prevention,
and we have to understand the timeline for

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:21.560
prevention must start much sooner.

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:26.660
You have to start doing these things long
before diseases are ravaging the system.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:33.230
The other big difference is that, as I talked
about it, the scientific method was such an

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:38.179
important part of Medicine 2.0, and with it
came something that I'm sure you've talked

00:33:38.179 --> 00:33:40.950
about many times, which is the randomized
controlled experiment.

00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:47.080
This is the tool that allowed us to do that
thing that Feynman described.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:50.280
The randomized controlled experiment is really
wonderful.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:54.210
It is insufficient by itself to solve the
problem.

00:33:54.210 --> 00:34:01.649
So Medicine 3.0 needs to go from what we call
evidence-based medicine, you only do what

00:34:01.649 --> 00:34:08.050
the randomized control trial says, to evidence-informed
medicine, which is, you take the insights

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:12.520
from the randomized control trials, but you
have to then modify them and specify them

00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:14.649
to a given patient.

00:34:14.649 --> 00:34:20.770
So those are really the huge leaps that we
have to take to now make this next transition.

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:26.740
Tim Ferriss: Could you say a bit more, expand
a bit, or give an example, hypothetical or

00:34:26.740 --> 00:34:36.050
otherwise, of moving from the — I don't
want to say sole, but extremely strong focus

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:42.320
on only making decisions for patients based
on RCT data to what you're describing?

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:45.120
Dr. Peter Attia: Exercise is a great example.

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:52.300
So exercise would say, okay, the recommendations
would be to — I'm going to give you two

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:53.300
examples, by the way.

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:56.710
So this one's kind of a simpler one, and then
a more nuanced, sort of nerdier one.

00:34:56.710 --> 00:35:03.930
But the nameplate recommendation around exercise
is something to the effect of two and a half,

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:07.010
three hours a week of moderately vigorous
activity.

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:13.180
But no one is parsing these data down and
actually looking at what metrics matter.

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:19.579
Like how much strength do you need to have
the absolute lowest risk in mortality?

00:35:19.579 --> 00:35:27.550
How much cardiorespiratory performance do
you need to absolutely lower your risk, literally

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:31.240
fourfold, relative to someone in the bottom
quartile?

00:35:31.240 --> 00:35:35.130
So do I have a randomized controlled experiment
that tells us that?

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:36.140
I don't.

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:41.610
No one's going to do that experiment, which
is, I'm going to take a group of 10,000 people,

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:46.680
randomize them to different levels of training,
increase each of their fitnesses to relative

00:35:46.680 --> 00:35:49.760
levels, and follow them prospectively for
the remainder of their lives.

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:51.260
That experiment can't be done.

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:56.650
So instead, we have to be able to triangulate
between these other sources of data.

00:35:56.650 --> 00:36:04.829
Another example, maybe a bit more glitchy,
might be the way we might use different forms

00:36:04.829 --> 00:36:07.250
of lipid-lowering medication.

00:36:07.250 --> 00:36:11.790
So this is kind of a whole other tangent we
could potentially dive into, which is if you're

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:17.280
really interested in preventing heart disease,
which by the way, number one cause of death

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:25.050
in men and women in the United States and
in the world, so it's the four-for-four champion

00:36:25.050 --> 00:36:26.240
of death.

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:32.079
It's such the champion of death that for every
woman who dies of breast cancer, there's like

00:36:32.079 --> 00:36:35.530
eight to 12 women who are dying of heart disease.

00:36:35.530 --> 00:36:40.869
So pink ribbons matter; red ribbons matter
more.

00:36:40.869 --> 00:36:46.280
This disease would be largely preventable
if people didn't smoke, controlled their blood

00:36:46.280 --> 00:36:53.880
pressure, and had apoB levels that were equivalent
to the levels they had as children.

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:58.740
No one will do the clinical trial that will
get us there, yet we have all the Mendelian

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:02.060
randomization, we have all the mechanistic
data.

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:08.720
And so an evidence-based approach says, look,
we're going to target LDL cholesterol to 100

00:37:08.720 --> 00:37:10.750
milligrams per deciliter.

00:37:10.750 --> 00:37:14.160
This approach is going to say, actually, that's
probably insufficient for this individual

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:19.700
because their risks are higher, and by the
way, their appetite for reducing the risk

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:20.920
of this disease is higher.

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:25.119
So we're going to take a more aggressive posture,
even though the recommendation in the literature

00:37:25.119 --> 00:37:27.800
is going to be far less aggressive.

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:37.100
Tim Ferriss: So Medicine 3.0 seems to comprise
a number of different elements, one of which

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:44.330
is early intervention or certainly much earlier
intervention, hence, early medical, I can

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:45.530
only imagine.

00:37:45.530 --> 00:37:57.560
And another is medical literacy and a more,
perhaps, proactive approach to education and

00:37:57.560 --> 00:37:58.910
intervention on the part of the patient.

00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:07.190
So it's not a passive experience of being
shepherded through a system by a doctor or

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:08.190
a team of doctors.

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:10.360
You take a much more active role.

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:15.460
And I wanted to chat about medical literacy
because what I've noticed, just to give a

00:38:15.460 --> 00:38:22.180
personal example, I long ago wanted to potentially
become a doctor and certainly became fascinated

00:38:22.180 --> 00:38:24.410
by competitive sports and performance enhancement.

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:30.300
And that led to doing my best to read studies
and dig into the science and understand as

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:32.339
much as possible.

00:38:32.339 --> 00:38:37.550
I was unsure of the value of that once I shifted
courses and decided to do other things.

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:42.382
But in the last, I would say, decade, as my
parents have gotten older, as more and more

00:38:42.382 --> 00:38:49.500
of my friends have run into issues, it is
astonishing how valuable this minimum effective

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:54.170
dose of medical and scientific literacy has
become.

00:38:54.170 --> 00:38:58.250
I mean, it's astonishing how much it helps
you to navigate things and make decisions

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:01.590
and parse signal from noise.

00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:10.240
So I'd love for you to speak to medical literacy
and how one can become more medically/scientifically

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:11.880
literate.

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:15.550
And certainly, I would highly, highly recommend,
I'll just double down on this, that everybody

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:19.160
read this book because I think that your book
does a good job of this, a very, very good

00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:20.339
job.

00:39:20.339 --> 00:39:26.180
And I'll just add maybe a sidebar note so
I can hear myself talk a little more.

00:39:26.180 --> 00:39:35.060
Which is that there's, I think, a very common
trend among people who are well-educated in

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:44.530
some areas, but not in medicine or science,
to succumb to Scientism with a capital S where

00:39:44.530 --> 00:39:52.460
they'll know the term randomized controlled
trial, and they will insist that if you say

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:58.220
anything outside of the purview of that, that
it is unsubstantiated nonsense.

00:39:58.220 --> 00:40:02.619
And there are other examples of this.

00:40:02.619 --> 00:40:05.190
Or they'll over-index on one thing, as you
mentioned.

00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:08.369
So pharmacology, it'll be either good or bad.

00:40:08.369 --> 00:40:13.500
But the fact is, anything totally over-indexed,
sort of the Paracelsus "The dose makes the

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:16.291
poison" type of problem.

00:40:16.291 --> 00:40:19.160
But could you speak to medical literacy?

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:20.720
Somebody's listening, they say, "You know
what?

00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:23.880
I recognize that I don't know much about this.

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:27.220
I would like to go about it in a really effective
way.

00:40:27.220 --> 00:40:28.220
How do I do it?"

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:31.119
Dr. Peter Attia: I think you and I have even
talked about this once before, Tim, and I

00:40:31.119 --> 00:40:34.960
don't think I had a great answer for you then,
and I don't know that I have a great answer

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:35.960
for you now.

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:40.340
It's really not a simple thing to do.

00:40:40.340 --> 00:40:42.750
Let's just start with the nomenclature.

00:40:42.750 --> 00:40:48.650
My first day of medical school, I still remember
one of the professors saying — and I don't

00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:51.990
remember the numbers, so the story is not
as cool because I don't remember the actual

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:52.990
numbers.

00:40:52.990 --> 00:40:54.120
But I'm making the numbers up, but not the
point, okay?

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:58.380
He said, "Well, all of you are smart hotshots
that just graduated the first of your class

00:40:58.380 --> 00:40:59.400
in college.

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:02.690
You have an average vocabulary of X words."

00:41:02.690 --> 00:41:04.520
And X was like, whatever number.

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:06.369
I honestly don't remember what it was.

00:41:06.369 --> 00:41:07.510
Call it 20,000 words.

00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:13.430
He said, "In the next two years, i.e., just
in the preclinical phase of medical school,

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:18.190
we are going to add Y words to your vocabulary."

00:41:18.190 --> 00:41:22.119
And Y was bigger than X by like 20 percent.

00:41:22.119 --> 00:41:25.430
And those were mostly going to be Latin and
Greek words, by the way.

00:41:25.430 --> 00:41:26.810
And he was right.

00:41:26.810 --> 00:41:29.250
I mean, it was like learning a new language.

00:41:29.250 --> 00:41:32.500
So part of it is just the semantic problem.

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:34.550
Now, do you have to know all of that?

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:36.670
Of course not.

00:41:36.670 --> 00:41:38.760
But what's proximal?

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:39.760
What's distal?

00:41:39.760 --> 00:41:42.289
All of these things kind of factor into this
stuff.

00:41:42.289 --> 00:41:48.119
By far the biggest issue, though, and this
is the one that we try to solve the most for

00:41:48.119 --> 00:41:54.000
on a weekly basis, there's a reason that we
go to great lengths to write a newsletter

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:55.670
every single Sunday.

00:41:55.670 --> 00:42:01.430
It's basically a class.

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:06.190
A lot of times it's like such-and-such a study
is published, and the news picks it up and

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:09.319
writes a take on it that we think is demonstrably
wrong.

00:42:09.319 --> 00:42:13.240
Or a paper gets published that gets a lot
of attention that says this, and we think

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:15.060
it's an important paper and we want to highlight
it.

00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:20.500
So we try to use those things as case studies,
and I honestly think that's the most efficient

00:42:20.500 --> 00:42:22.170
way to go about learning this.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:27.920
Now, we did write a series six years ago,
I think, called Studying Studies, which is

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:32.760
I think a five-part blog series that Bob Kaplan
and I wrote.

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:39.000
And I think it's probably a great foundation
to just get the basics.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:41.119
What is a case cohort study?

00:42:41.119 --> 00:42:43.000
What is observational epidemiology?

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:46.900
What types of bias exist, and how do you look
for them?

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:53.700
Consider that sort of your basic foundational
knowledge set, but thereafter it becomes reps.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:57.400
I think Layne Norton also has a course that
he just launched, and I think it's actually

00:42:57.400 --> 00:43:00.640
called REPS, but I can't remember what REPS
stands for.

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:05.240
But it's basically a scientific literacy course
in how to read studies about nutrition.

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:08.010
In his case, it's about exercise and nutrition.

00:43:08.010 --> 00:43:11.329
Again, we try to cover everything in our newsletter.

00:43:11.329 --> 00:43:14.380
We'll talk about a drug study, we'll talk
about a supplement study, an exercise study,

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:15.900
all those sorts of things.

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:19.560
So those to me are the ways you go about doing
this.

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:24.980
You just have to dive into it and a little
bit overcome the fear of it.

00:43:24.980 --> 00:43:31.420
I suspect it's not unlike what you would say
if I was asking you, "Tim, I really want to

00:43:31.420 --> 00:43:32.420
learn Japanese.

00:43:32.420 --> 00:43:34.319
Is there a particular app that I should look
at?"

00:43:34.319 --> 00:43:38.780
I think at the end of the day, you'd say,
"Look, man, you've got to learn it.

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:42.670
Here's the way to get the foundational skills,
and then go and find people to speak Japanese

00:43:42.670 --> 00:43:43.670
with."

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:45.819
Tim Ferriss: Well, let's actually use that
example.

00:43:45.819 --> 00:43:46.819
So learning Japanese.

00:43:46.819 --> 00:43:49.700
Because I do think there are parallels here.

00:43:49.700 --> 00:43:52.930
I think it comes back to objectives, strategy,
and tactics.

00:43:52.930 --> 00:43:57.059
And I just wanted to share a quote that's
one of my favorite quotes that also might

00:43:57.059 --> 00:44:00.520
help folks, which is from Ralph Waldo Emerson.

00:44:00.520 --> 00:44:04.059
And the quote is, "The man who grasps," this
is, of course, people, but "The man who grasps

00:44:04.059 --> 00:44:07.390
principles can successfully select his own
methods.

00:44:07.390 --> 00:44:10.869
The man who tries methods, ignoring principles,
is sure to have trouble."

00:44:10.869 --> 00:44:19.450
This applies as much in medicine, developing
scientific literacy or learning Japanese.

00:44:19.450 --> 00:44:21.390
It applies in all of these places.

00:44:21.390 --> 00:44:28.460
And so what I would say, and please feel free
to pick this apart, is that to learn Japanese

00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:30.760
is a very broad objective.

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:35.160
So I would encourage someone to refine that
substantially.

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:43.460
And they might refine it to, I would like
to be conversationally fluent, which means

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:52.319
I understand and can convey maybe 80 percent
of what I hear or want to say, in a conversation

00:44:52.319 --> 00:44:59.480
that does not include specialist terminology
that is abstract, like economics or art, fill

00:44:59.480 --> 00:45:00.829
in the blank.

00:45:00.829 --> 00:45:04.400
So you're discussing what happened to whom,
when.

00:45:04.400 --> 00:45:09.450
I was born here, I went to school there, I
have two brothers, whatever it might be.

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:15.490
And with that goal, then I would say for the
vast majority of people, if, and this is big

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:26.310
if, they were willing to make it a part-time
job, let's just say with three or four hours

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:32.410
per week spread across four consecutive days.

00:45:32.410 --> 00:45:39.170
That if they layered things properly, within
three to six months, if they then added in

00:45:39.170 --> 00:45:45.050
a couple of larger sessions for bulk memorization,
and you can use space repetition tools, but

00:45:45.050 --> 00:45:50.410
the tools don't matter as much as being really
clear on the objective, that you could become

00:45:50.410 --> 00:45:54.510
functionally fluent, conversationally fluent
as I described it, which omits reading and

00:45:54.510 --> 00:46:00.190
writing completely in Japanese scripts, probably
within three to six months if you took it

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:01.190
super seriously.

00:46:01.190 --> 00:46:03.230
And there are some adjuncts I would add to
that.

00:46:03.230 --> 00:46:08.420
So to give people maybe a glimmer of hope
on the medical literacy side, I would say

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:16.309
if people started with your AMA number 30,
this is episode 188, "How to Read and Understand

00:46:16.309 --> 00:46:17.450
Scientific Studies."

00:46:17.450 --> 00:46:22.569
If people listen to that first, and assume,
folks, before you listen to it that you're

00:46:22.569 --> 00:46:25.550
not going to understand at least 30 percent.

00:46:25.550 --> 00:46:28.950
Let's just make that an expectation so you
don't get frustrated.

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:35.050
And if they further maybe read some of the
case studies from your newsletter, made that

00:46:35.050 --> 00:46:38.860
one of the hours that they spend per week,
just like the Japanese.

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:44.940
If they maybe read a book like Bad Science
by Ben Goldacre, who's an MD.

00:46:44.940 --> 00:46:52.030
British, so some of the cultural context and
examples may not resonate for US readers,

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:53.800
but he runs through some of the basics.

00:46:53.800 --> 00:47:02.619
And they read Outlive, that making this literacy
your part-time job, a very light part-time

00:47:02.619 --> 00:47:10.450
job, for three months is one of the best investments
you'll ever make in your life.

00:47:10.450 --> 00:47:20.109
I mean, I really feel like the payoff is so
tremendous that it seems almost crazy to not

00:47:20.109 --> 00:47:21.109
learn.

00:47:21.109 --> 00:47:23.300
And you don't have to learn a hundred thousand
words, but you need to be familiar with, let's

00:47:23.300 --> 00:47:30.460
just call it a few dozen concepts, that you'll
be able to look at databases of studies, or

00:47:30.460 --> 00:47:35.160
at least abstracts, not going into all the
supplemental stuff, and make some sense of

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:39.109
A, B, or C.
Are there any suggestions you would have for

00:47:39.109 --> 00:47:43.819
just becoming familiar for with what the scientific
method actually entails?

00:47:43.819 --> 00:47:47.790
So I could think, read the Wikipedia entry
as a starting point.

00:47:47.790 --> 00:47:53.630
But for instance, where a lot of smart people
or seemingly smart people screw up with the

00:47:53.630 --> 00:47:58.610
scientific method, is your guess, although
it is a guess, aka the hypothesis, needs to

00:47:58.610 --> 00:47:59.640
be falsifiable.

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:04.290
And a lot of people start with completely
impossible-to-falsify guesses.

00:48:04.290 --> 00:48:08.369
And it's just you're painting yourself up
shit's creek with without a paddle.

00:48:08.369 --> 00:48:13.410
Any suggestions for how people could at least
become familiar with the scientific method

00:48:13.410 --> 00:48:18.250
and what that means as far as structured thought
and process?

00:48:18.250 --> 00:48:22.359
Dr. Peter Attia: I don't off the top of my
head have a resource that I can point to.

00:48:22.359 --> 00:48:28.670
I agree with you that there's so much more
nuance to it than just understanding the caricatured

00:48:28.670 --> 00:48:31.460
version that I explained earlier.

00:48:31.460 --> 00:48:34.670
For example, how you design an experiment
is essential.

00:48:34.670 --> 00:48:40.180
So even if you have a hypothesis that can
be falsified, if you design the wrong experiment

00:48:40.180 --> 00:48:43.640
to test it, you can miss out.

00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:46.880
This becomes very important, by the way, as
you start to look at studies.

00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:52.579
If a study is "negative," how do you know
if it's negative because it was too small

00:48:52.579 --> 00:48:59.490
in size and what we would describe as underpowered,
versus it was adequately powered, but there

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:00.490
was no effect.

00:49:00.490 --> 00:49:06.390
At the other end of that spectrum, sometimes
a study will find a statistical significance,

00:49:06.390 --> 00:49:10.910
but it was overpowered, and that difference,
while statistically significant is clinically

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:11.910
irrelevant.

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:16.020
Now, that kind of stuff we do cover in the
Studying Studies stuff.

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:22.910
But I think what you're describing are these
more important heuristics that truthfully,

00:49:22.910 --> 00:49:26.329
maybe there is a great book out there, and
I'm just ignorant of what that book is that

00:49:26.329 --> 00:49:30.480
goes through this, but that's the kind of
stuff that I think you learn with practice.

00:49:30.480 --> 00:49:34.520
And unfortunately, if you're not doing experiments
— I mean, again, I think I learned most

00:49:34.520 --> 00:49:41.120
of this stuff not in medicine, but in the
two years that I spent at the NIH where I

00:49:41.120 --> 00:49:42.750
was a fellow, but doing research.

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:47.020
So I was a research fellow, not a clinician.

00:49:47.020 --> 00:49:53.090
That's probably where I learned more than
anything about how to do science.

00:49:53.090 --> 00:49:56.930
And so I think anybody who's been in a lab
is just learning that.

00:49:56.930 --> 00:49:58.970
But it's very informal.

00:49:58.970 --> 00:50:02.240
At least for me, it wasn't a structured thing.

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:07.390
Tim Ferriss: But in terms of priorities, I
would say, folks, man, I've seen so many people

00:50:07.390 --> 00:50:13.900
lost in the medical system, especially when
they suddenly have a serious diagnosis with

00:50:13.900 --> 00:50:15.760
some form of cancer or otherwise.

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:21.960
And they are absolutely helpless because they
do not have a handful of concepts that they've

00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:25.930
become comfortable with and just a basic set
of terminology.

00:50:25.930 --> 00:50:28.119
So at the very least, look up Sir Francis
Bacon.

00:50:28.119 --> 00:50:32.050
I'm sure there's a biography, an excellent
biography out there somewhere.

00:50:32.050 --> 00:50:38.130
And it will no doubt track the development
of the, I guess, Baconian approach.

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:43.369
Dr. Peter Attia: One thing I'll add to this,
Tim, before we leave this topic, is also for

00:50:43.369 --> 00:50:47.140
folks to look up Austin Bradford Hill.

00:50:47.140 --> 00:50:54.099
So Austin Bradford Hill, scientist of the
middle part of the 20th century.

00:50:54.099 --> 00:50:57.940
To him, we owe the Bradford Hill criteria.

00:50:57.940 --> 00:51:02.850
So there are, I believe, nine Bradford Hill
criteria, which are the criteria that we use

00:51:02.850 --> 00:51:05.550
to scrutinize observational data.

00:51:05.550 --> 00:51:13.990
So when you have epidemiologic data, how can
you scrutinize them to understand and change

00:51:13.990 --> 00:51:17.380
your confidence in the likelihood that these
data are causal?

00:51:17.380 --> 00:51:21.680
I mean, causality is one of the most important
concepts in science.

00:51:21.680 --> 00:51:27.120
Tim Ferriss: Could you also just define observational
study versus randomized control trial so that

00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:28.120
people have that?

00:51:28.120 --> 00:51:29.120
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

00:51:29.120 --> 00:51:32.290
So an observational study, it doesn't have
an intervention.

00:51:32.290 --> 00:51:36.599
So you can observe things, either looking
backwards, so things that already happened,

00:51:36.599 --> 00:51:38.599
you can go back and look at what happened.

00:51:38.599 --> 00:51:42.180
You can even observe things prospectively,
but without intervening.

00:51:42.180 --> 00:51:47.671
So saying, hey, we noticed that there are
a group of redheads over here and there are

00:51:47.671 --> 00:51:50.780
a group of brunettes over here, and we're
just going to follow them for the next 10

00:51:50.780 --> 00:51:56.160
years and see if the color of their hair predicts
their life satisfaction, making this up, of

00:51:56.160 --> 00:51:57.160
course.

00:51:57.160 --> 00:52:03.240
And so you might get an answer from that and
say, oh, my God, the people with red hair

00:52:03.240 --> 00:52:05.630
were so much more likely to get melanoma.

00:52:05.630 --> 00:52:13.099
I mean, this is devastating, and it must be
that something about red hair is causing skin

00:52:13.099 --> 00:52:14.099
cancer.

00:52:14.099 --> 00:52:19.170
And it must be that brown hair must be reflecting
the sun and those people aren't getting skin

00:52:19.170 --> 00:52:20.670
cancer.

00:52:20.670 --> 00:52:21.670
So there's a correlation.

00:52:21.670 --> 00:52:25.099
So another question is, do we know if there's
a cause there?

00:52:25.099 --> 00:52:29.400
Do we know if the red hair is causing those
people to get more skin cancer than the people

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:30.400
with brown hair?

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:31.780
And the answer is probably not, right?

00:52:31.780 --> 00:52:36.800
The answer is probably that red hair is associated
with fairer skin and that that's the issue.

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:39.599
Because if you really wanted to know if red
hair was causal, you would randomize people

00:52:39.599 --> 00:52:44.190
to different groups and you would actually
dye the hair in one group one color, and dye

00:52:44.190 --> 00:52:46.970
the hair in the other group another color,
and then you would follow them.

00:52:46.970 --> 00:52:47.970
You see?

00:52:47.970 --> 00:52:53.060
So what Bradford Hill's criteria allow us
to do is look at nine factors, such as the

00:52:53.060 --> 00:52:54.799
strength of the observation.

00:52:54.799 --> 00:52:57.859
So what's the actual magnitude of the observation?

00:52:57.859 --> 00:52:59.520
What's the reproducibility of it?

00:52:59.520 --> 00:53:01.920
What's the biological plausibility of it?

00:53:01.920 --> 00:53:03.390
Is there an analogy to it?

00:53:03.390 --> 00:53:05.780
Is there a dose effect to it?

00:53:05.780 --> 00:53:09.500
And as you go through these nine criteria,
which I think I actually do this a couple

00:53:09.500 --> 00:53:14.040
of times in the book, because so much of the
data we look at around nutrition and exercise

00:53:14.040 --> 00:53:16.030
are unfortunately epidemiological.

00:53:16.030 --> 00:53:18.140
We can't do the controlled experiments.

00:53:18.140 --> 00:53:24.520
But it's what allows us to say, when I read
a study that says 12 hazelnuts will reduce

00:53:24.520 --> 00:53:29.760
your risk of death, 12 hazelnuts a day in
consumption reduces risk of death by 10 percent,

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:32.520
I can look at that and say, total nonsense.

00:53:32.520 --> 00:53:34.849
That's just epidemiologic nonsense.

00:53:34.849 --> 00:53:39.780
Conversely, when I look at epidemiologic data
that say, people who are really, really strong

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:44.140
live longer than people who are really, really
weak, by going through those nine criteria,

00:53:44.140 --> 00:53:47.109
I can be much more confident that that is
actually causal.

00:53:47.109 --> 00:53:51.140
Tim Ferriss: I'll mention two things.

00:53:51.140 --> 00:53:54.160
The first is if you're going to get — because
we're mentioning a number of different books,

00:53:54.160 --> 00:53:58.599
and this is not just to blows smoke up Peter's
ass, which by the way was a real thing.

00:53:58.599 --> 00:54:03.420
I found a menu from the wild west, and it
was all these various things you could have

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:05.540
done to you, and blowing smoke up your ass
was like $50.

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:06.540
It was the most expensive.

00:54:06.540 --> 00:54:07.540
So that's a real thing.

00:54:07.540 --> 00:54:12.270
So it's not just that I'm doing that, but
if you're going to buy one book on this stuff,

00:54:12.270 --> 00:54:15.240
get Outlive because it covers a broad spectrum.

00:54:15.240 --> 00:54:19.640
And I want to also mention, because I think
this is really critical, and we'll probably

00:54:19.640 --> 00:54:26.150
come back to this, but it isn't just about
extending lifespan, but extending healthspan

00:54:26.150 --> 00:54:32.070
and having full optionality physically, or
trying to preserve that to the greatest extent

00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:34.200
possible for the longest period of time possible.

00:54:34.200 --> 00:54:41.470
I will add, though, that people will see,
for instance, and a lot of this medical or

00:54:41.470 --> 00:54:46.730
scientific literacy is so you don't get fooled
by misinterpretation or sensationalism in

00:54:46.730 --> 00:54:47.730
media.

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:54.160
Because you'll see a headline that's something
like "Bananas increase your risk of colorectal

00:54:54.160 --> 00:54:56.559
cancer by a hundred percent."

00:54:56.559 --> 00:55:01.339
And what that means, though, if you dig into
the data is like, all right, your risk goes

00:55:01.339 --> 00:55:04.990
from one in five billion to two in five billion.

00:55:04.990 --> 00:55:10.060
So it's just like, from a behavioral standpoint,
it just does not matter at all.

00:55:10.060 --> 00:55:15.260
There's a book called How to Lie With Statistics
by Darrell Huff, which I think is a very fast

00:55:15.260 --> 00:55:16.260
read.

00:55:16.260 --> 00:55:19.690
And that will also complement everything that
we're talking about.

00:55:19.690 --> 00:55:28.349
So let's look a little more closely at some
of the content and interventions and tools

00:55:28.349 --> 00:55:30.869
in the book.

00:55:30.869 --> 00:55:36.160
And I'm not sure this is a great way to kick
it off, but there's an expression I've heard

00:55:36.160 --> 00:55:40.339
at least applied to medicine quite a few times,
which is 50 percent of what we know is wrong,

00:55:40.339 --> 00:55:42.579
we just don't know which 50 percent.

00:55:42.579 --> 00:55:51.829
And so I'm curious to know what you have seen,
peeking behind the veil at what is not working,

00:55:51.829 --> 00:56:02.079
and what you have then designed to address
that misfire, in your practice and by extension

00:56:02.079 --> 00:56:03.079
in the book.

00:56:03.079 --> 00:56:05.240
You mentioned a few things, like apoB levels.

00:56:05.240 --> 00:56:10.640
You mentioned being very specific with exercise
prescription, being extremely specific, and

00:56:10.640 --> 00:56:15.059
the possibility of lowering certain types
of risks fourfold.

00:56:15.059 --> 00:56:17.650
So I'll let you tackle that however you want.

00:56:17.650 --> 00:56:18.890
We can start wherever makes sense.

00:56:18.890 --> 00:56:22.369
Dr. Peter Attia: Well, I mean, we could probably
talk about different things within different

00:56:22.369 --> 00:56:23.369
buckets.

00:56:23.369 --> 00:56:29.619
So starting with sleep, I think that with
sleep, I like to simplify things a little

00:56:29.619 --> 00:56:31.049
bit sometimes.

00:56:31.049 --> 00:56:39.680
So one thing that I think is happening a lot
right now is people are getting, probably

00:56:39.680 --> 00:56:44.039
at least some people overly anxious about
what their sleep trackers are telling them.

00:56:44.039 --> 00:56:49.710
So we've got a problem where the pendulum
has swung a little too far to the other side,

00:56:49.710 --> 00:56:54.700
where maybe 10 years ago, nobody thought at
all about sleep.

00:56:54.700 --> 00:57:00.650
Now you've got a class of people, me among
them, who maybe think too much about sleep.

00:57:00.650 --> 00:57:03.049
All of a sudden, it becomes a little bit counterproductive.

00:57:03.049 --> 00:57:05.799
We see this in a subset of our patients.

00:57:05.799 --> 00:57:09.450
The first and most important prescription
for those people is stop tracking.

00:57:09.450 --> 00:57:12.400
Take the tracker off.

00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:14.750
Let's just focus on the fundamentals.

00:57:14.750 --> 00:57:17.990
What time are you getting into bed, and what
time are you getting out of bed?

00:57:17.990 --> 00:57:25.559
If we can, within 15 to 30 minutes on either
one of those, completely fix that and completely

00:57:25.559 --> 00:57:30.661
toggle on the basic blocking and tackling
aspects of sleep hygiene, which have to do

00:57:30.661 --> 00:57:37.869
with food, alcohol, light, temperature, noise,
et cetera, I mean, you're going to be okay.

00:57:37.869 --> 00:57:42.160
I don't really need to know what your sleep
score is and how many minutes of this sleep

00:57:42.160 --> 00:57:44.359
versus that sleep you had.

00:57:44.359 --> 00:57:46.000
I personally go through this myself.

00:57:46.000 --> 00:57:49.680
I'll go through phases of time where I'm not
looking at any sleep-tracking device.

00:57:49.680 --> 00:57:54.180
If I know I'm going to be in an environment
where sleep is compromised, which is basically

00:57:54.180 --> 00:57:58.539
a lot of the time if I'm traveling, I don't
even take a sleep tracker with me because

00:57:58.539 --> 00:58:01.270
why do I need something to remind me of what's
probably happening?

00:58:01.270 --> 00:58:05.230
How about I just do the best job I can and
be a little less data focused?

00:58:05.230 --> 00:58:10.839
That's an example of where I feel like, a
couple years ago, I would've just said, "More

00:58:10.839 --> 00:58:12.540
data is always the answer."

00:58:12.540 --> 00:58:16.789
Now I think I'm a bit more measured, and I
say, "Look, sometimes more data is not always

00:58:16.789 --> 00:58:17.789
the answer."

00:58:17.789 --> 00:58:21.940
Now, the flip side of that is there's nothing
that will demonstrate to a person how much

00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:25.369
late night food and alcohol are ruining their
sleep than a sleep tracker.

00:58:25.369 --> 00:58:29.450
I do think it's really valuable to see that.

00:58:29.450 --> 00:58:34.570
For many of my patients, that has been the
most decisive thing that has reduced their

00:58:34.570 --> 00:58:35.570
alcohol intake.

00:58:35.570 --> 00:58:36.570
Tim Ferriss: Oh, for sure.

00:58:36.570 --> 00:58:37.570
Yes.

00:58:37.570 --> 00:58:39.430
Dr. Peter Attia: Despite what a lot of the
epidemiology will tell people, alcohol is

00:58:39.430 --> 00:58:40.970
not good for you in any dose.

00:58:40.970 --> 00:58:44.170
It doesn't mean we shouldn't drink it at all,
but let's not delude ourselves into thinking

00:58:44.170 --> 00:58:46.130
it's actually healthy at some low dose.

00:58:46.130 --> 00:58:47.320
It's not.

00:58:47.320 --> 00:58:51.100
Understanding that it's a toxin can, I think,
help you have a more measured response.

00:58:51.100 --> 00:58:55.400
My response to alcohol, while we're on that
topic, is if I drink something that doesn't

00:58:55.400 --> 00:58:58.400
taste incredible, I pour it out.

00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:01.809
I'm never going to tolerate a bad glass of
wine, ever.

00:59:01.809 --> 00:59:03.310
It's just not worth it.

00:59:03.310 --> 00:59:09.250
Tim Ferriss: Since we're on it, well, I'm
going to pull us back to this question of

00:59:09.250 --> 00:59:16.670
data, probably through the lens of continuous
glucose monitors and the best uses and maybe

00:59:16.670 --> 00:59:22.930
the most common misuses for people who do
not have type two or type one diabetes.

00:59:22.930 --> 00:59:27.549
But let's just sit with the alcohol for a
second.

00:59:27.549 --> 00:59:35.589
What are your personal rules for alcohol consumption,
outside of if it tastes mediocre or shitty,

00:59:35.589 --> 00:59:39.319
it gets poured out, which is a great rule,
but what are your favorite types of alcohol?

00:59:39.319 --> 00:59:40.940
How do you personally navigate that?

00:59:40.940 --> 00:59:42.869
Dr. Peter Attia: Well, I'll start with quantity.

00:59:42.869 --> 00:59:43.869
Yeah.

00:59:43.869 --> 00:59:49.059
I would personally just say there's got to
be an insane reason to have more than two

00:59:49.059 --> 00:59:51.619
drinks in a day.

00:59:51.619 --> 00:59:53.860
It's somewhere between zero and two.

00:59:53.860 --> 00:59:58.190
And it's got to be a really good reason to
drink on more than three days a week.

00:59:58.190 --> 01:00:01.680
In the back of my mind, I'm keeping a tally,
which is I really shouldn't be having more

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:04.640
than about seven drinks in a week.

01:00:04.640 --> 01:00:09.990
Again, seven drinks in one day is very different
than one drink a day for seven days.

01:00:09.990 --> 01:00:13.630
It's the frequency and dose that defines the
poison.

01:00:13.630 --> 01:00:19.809
The second thing is I really have to make
sure that drinking is a good three hours away

01:00:19.809 --> 01:00:21.000
from sleep.

01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:24.609
Again, when I say these things, people say,
"Peter, you must be a robot."

01:00:24.609 --> 01:00:26.970
No, I'm just saying these are general principles.

01:00:26.970 --> 01:00:28.661
There are going to be times when I violate
that.

01:00:28.661 --> 01:00:32.630
I think I posted something on Instagram a
little while ago, which was the most rancid

01:00:32.630 --> 01:00:35.960
night of sleep, when my sleep data were comically
bad.

01:00:35.960 --> 01:00:38.220
The reason was we had friends over.

01:00:38.220 --> 01:00:40.200
It was very late.

01:00:40.200 --> 01:00:41.200
We drank.

01:00:41.200 --> 01:00:42.210
We ate.

01:00:42.210 --> 01:00:43.319
I basically went to bed.

01:00:43.319 --> 01:00:47.030
By the time they left at 10:30, I went to
bed, and we had just finished eating.

01:00:47.030 --> 01:00:48.940
Not surprisingly, my sleep sucked.

01:00:48.940 --> 01:00:52.160
The point I made in the post was I'd do it
again.

01:00:52.160 --> 01:00:55.049
It was a fantastic night with our friends.

01:00:55.049 --> 01:00:58.240
The four of us hadn't had dinner together
in a long time.

01:00:58.240 --> 01:01:02.609
You just have to be very thoughtful and deliberate
about the choices and trade-offs that you

01:01:02.609 --> 01:01:03.609
make.

01:01:03.609 --> 01:01:10.910
As far as the type of alcohol, this is, again,
an example of something I think we tend to

01:01:10.910 --> 01:01:12.270
over-index to.

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:16.350
It's "I drink a lot, but let me tell you,
it's just vodka and tequila.

01:01:16.350 --> 01:01:18.180
It's got to be good for me, right?"

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:19.780
It's like, "No.

01:01:19.780 --> 01:01:20.849
Alcohol's alcohol."

01:01:20.849 --> 01:01:21.849
I love tequila.

01:01:21.849 --> 01:01:22.849
I love mezcal.

01:01:22.849 --> 01:01:25.170
I love really good wine.

01:01:25.170 --> 01:01:27.820
I love really dark Belgian beer.

01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:31.380
I'm not deluded to think that any of those
are healthy.

01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:35.470
I know that there are some people who have
a horrible reaction to certain types of alcohol.

01:01:35.470 --> 01:01:37.960
Well, I would put that in the same category
as people who have a horrible reaction to

01:01:37.960 --> 01:01:39.290
certain types of foods.

01:01:39.290 --> 01:01:41.369
Don't consume them.

01:01:41.369 --> 01:01:46.770
I think mostly where I focus, Tim, is on how
much am I going to drink, how close is it

01:01:46.770 --> 01:01:51.400
going to be to bed, and what's the total tally
per week, and never exceed a certain tally

01:01:51.400 --> 01:01:52.400
in a given day.

01:01:52.400 --> 01:01:56.799
Tim Ferriss: I was waiting for the right time
to bring this up, Peter I know that people

01:01:56.799 --> 01:01:59.849
say cookies are bad for you, but I've only
been eating Nutter Butters.

01:01:59.849 --> 01:02:00.849
I'm kidding.

01:02:00.849 --> 01:02:03.369
I feel like that's the equivalent although
it's less of a neurotoxin.

01:02:03.369 --> 01:02:08.119
Let's hop to continuous —
Dr. Peter Attia: I do love Nutter Butters,

01:02:08.119 --> 01:02:09.119
by the way.

01:02:09.119 --> 01:02:10.119
Tim Ferriss: They are good.

01:02:10.119 --> 01:02:11.119
They're so good.

01:02:11.119 --> 01:02:13.170
Dr. Peter Attia: I don't know the last time
I had one, but oh, my God, now that you reminded

01:02:13.170 --> 01:02:14.170
me of that.

01:02:14.170 --> 01:02:15.190
That's horrible of you to do that.

01:02:15.190 --> 01:02:24.220
Tim Ferriss: Well, that'll combine nicely
with all the GBs and the CGMs.

01:02:24.220 --> 01:02:25.220
Inside joke.

01:02:25.220 --> 01:02:30.099
Could you speak to continuous glucose monitors?

01:02:30.099 --> 01:02:37.880
For a long time, I mean, even still now, these
are broadly thought of as something that one

01:02:37.880 --> 01:02:42.430
might only use if they have some form of diabetes.

01:02:42.430 --> 01:02:47.570
However, there are people who do not fit in
those categories who use them.

01:02:47.570 --> 01:02:53.490
I would love for you to just perhaps give
us a bit of an overview on CGMs and what you

01:02:53.490 --> 01:02:56.559
have seen as the best uses and how you use
them in your practice.

01:02:56.559 --> 01:03:00.400
Dr. Peter Attia: A continuous glucose monitor
is a device.

01:03:00.400 --> 01:03:04.029
There are really two big ones on the market
right now.

01:03:04.029 --> 01:03:08.230
One is made by a company called Dexcom, and
one is made by a company called Abbott.

01:03:08.230 --> 01:03:10.380
The latter is called the Freestyle Libre.

01:03:10.380 --> 01:03:13.190
I think it's a company that Abbott acquired
many years ago.

01:03:13.190 --> 01:03:17.040
These are devices that are worn usually on
the tricep.

01:03:17.040 --> 01:03:19.490
They can also be on the abdomen.

01:03:19.490 --> 01:03:26.020
They have a little, tiny filament that is
quickly shot in with a needle into the subcutaneous

01:03:26.020 --> 01:03:27.020
space.

01:03:27.020 --> 01:03:31.931
The filament remains after the needle comes
out, and the filament sits in a part of the

01:03:31.931 --> 01:03:34.819
body where it can sample something called
interstitial fluid.

01:03:34.819 --> 01:03:37.599
This is basically the fluid between cells.

01:03:37.599 --> 01:03:42.740
To be clear, it's not sampling the blood.

01:03:42.740 --> 01:03:47.910
It samples the interstitial fluid, and it
measures glucose level there.

01:03:47.910 --> 01:03:52.630
It's basically calibrated to know, based on
what the reading is there, what the glucose

01:03:52.630 --> 01:03:54.260
level is in the blood.

01:03:54.260 --> 01:04:01.300
A continuous glucose monitor, as its name
suggests, is a way to give you real-time information

01:04:01.300 --> 01:04:04.119
about what your blood glucose is.

01:04:04.119 --> 01:04:10.680
As you said, for people with type one diabetes,
which was the first use case, it was a very

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:15.160
important innovation because people with type
one diabetes are by definition dependent on

01:04:15.160 --> 01:04:16.700
insulin.

01:04:16.700 --> 01:04:22.410
They are using insulin exogenously to do the
job of their pancreas, which is no longer

01:04:22.410 --> 01:04:24.640
working.

01:04:24.640 --> 01:04:29.049
The pancreas, of course, is this brilliant
organ, where it's the one that's auto regulating

01:04:29.049 --> 01:04:31.109
glucose and insulin, or glucose via insulin.

01:04:31.109 --> 01:04:33.869
When that goes away, they have to be the one
to do it.

01:04:33.869 --> 01:04:38.119
So now they have this device that is at least
giving them real-time glucose information,

01:04:38.119 --> 01:04:43.530
and they can adjust insulin injections as
needed.

01:04:43.530 --> 01:04:49.230
It obviously didn't take long for that to
expand into a much, much larger market, i.e.,

01:04:49.230 --> 01:04:55.670
10 times the size, which is people with type
two diabetes, some of whom do require exogenous

01:04:55.670 --> 01:05:02.270
insulin, some of whom do not, but all of whom
by definition have a carbohydrate and glucose

01:05:02.270 --> 01:05:03.420
tolerance disorder.

01:05:03.420 --> 01:05:13.390
The very definition of type two diabetes is
based around unregulated peripheral glucose.

01:05:13.390 --> 01:05:15.450
This tool is therefore helpful.

01:05:15.450 --> 01:05:20.380
It's helpful in, first and foremost, I think,
knowing what to eat.

01:05:20.380 --> 01:05:26.359
I think, historically, we have given people
with type two diabetes abjectly horrible news

01:05:26.359 --> 01:05:28.930
and insight with regard to what to eat.

01:05:28.930 --> 01:05:33.150
I mean, we just haven't really helped them
think through this problem.

01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:38.950
The CGM makes it pretty clear that not all
foods are created equal when it comes to managing

01:05:38.950 --> 01:05:41.870
glucose homeostasis.

01:05:41.870 --> 01:05:45.309
Everything I've said so far is relatively
straightforward.

01:05:45.309 --> 01:05:49.120
This is a great example, by the way, of Medicine
3.0 versus Medicine 2.0.

01:05:49.120 --> 01:05:52.710
Everything I've said so far more or less makes
sense if you're in Medicine 2.0.

01:05:52.710 --> 01:05:57.270
In fact, I think we're finally at the point
where even Medicine 2.0 is starting to accept

01:05:57.270 --> 01:06:01.529
the fact that carbohydrate restriction is
a really good idea for people with type two

01:06:01.529 --> 01:06:02.529
diabetes.

01:06:02.529 --> 01:06:05.310
It's not the only way, by the way.

01:06:05.310 --> 01:06:10.180
Caloric restriction in general will work to
improve insulin sensitivity, which therefore

01:06:10.180 --> 01:06:12.630
improves glucose regulation.

01:06:12.630 --> 01:06:21.490
But it does seem that carbohydrate restriction,
as a form or a gateway to caloric restriction,

01:06:21.490 --> 01:06:26.700
has greater efficacy than other methods.

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:32.910
Once we now talk about people without diabetes,
Medicine 2.0 would argue a CGM plays no role.

01:06:32.910 --> 01:06:33.910
Okay.

01:06:33.910 --> 01:06:39.960
Is there a randomized control trial demonstrating
the efficacy of CGM in anything outside of

01:06:39.960 --> 01:06:40.960
patients with diabetes?

01:06:40.960 --> 01:06:41.960
There is not.

01:06:41.960 --> 01:06:46.869
But now we have to get into the leap of faith
that one takes when you start triangulating

01:06:46.869 --> 01:06:48.349
between other pieces of data.

01:06:48.349 --> 01:06:54.930
We want to start with one, which is, in a
population of non-diabetics, is there any

01:06:54.930 --> 01:06:58.880
evidence that glucose levels matter?

01:06:58.880 --> 01:07:04.790
Type two diabetes is defined as having a hemoglobin
A1C above 6.5 percent.

01:07:04.790 --> 01:07:09.320
Hemoglobin A1C is just a way to measure how
much glucose is stuck to hemoglobin.

01:07:09.320 --> 01:07:13.810
By knowing what that number is, you can impute
what the average blood glucose is in that

01:07:13.810 --> 01:07:15.970
individual over the preceding three months.

01:07:15.970 --> 01:07:21.589
So 6.5, which is the cutoff, translates to
an average blood glucose of 140 milligrams

01:07:21.589 --> 01:07:22.589
per deciliter.

01:07:22.589 --> 01:07:31.020
The question, then, would be if you take two
people who don't have diabetes, one of whom

01:07:31.020 --> 01:07:40.690
has a hemoglobin A1C of 5.0, and one of whom
has a hemoglobin A1C of 6.0, neither of them

01:07:40.690 --> 01:07:44.619
are diabetic, is there any difference in their
outcomes?

01:07:44.619 --> 01:07:50.750
To put that in perspective, the guy at 5.0
has an average blood glucose of about 100

01:07:50.750 --> 01:07:52.420
milligrams per deciliter.

01:07:52.420 --> 01:07:55.340
The person at 6.0 has an average blood glucose
of about 130 milligrams per deciliter.

01:07:55.340 --> 01:07:56.340
Pardon me.

01:07:56.340 --> 01:07:57.340
120 milligrams per deciliter.

01:07:57.340 --> 01:07:58.510
They're both outside that diabetes range.

01:07:58.510 --> 01:08:00.589
Well, the answer here is actually pretty clear.

01:08:00.589 --> 01:08:02.220
It is that there is a difference.

01:08:02.220 --> 01:08:08.799
In fact, for a non-diabetic population, the
lower the average blood glucose, as estimated

01:08:08.799 --> 01:08:13.620
by hemoglobin a1c, the lower the all-cause
mortality.

01:08:13.620 --> 01:08:14.620
What's the implication now?

01:08:14.620 --> 01:08:18.600
Well, if the implication is, if you take a
group of people who don't have diabetes but

01:08:18.600 --> 01:08:22.500
want to live longer, i.e., they want to lower
their risk of cancer, they want to lower their

01:08:22.500 --> 01:08:26.570
risk of cardiovascular disease, they want
to lower their risk of neurodegenerative disease,

01:08:26.570 --> 01:08:31.600
I think we can make a pretty reasonable leap
of faith that having a lower average blood

01:08:31.600 --> 01:08:36.009
glucose is better than having a higher average
blood glucose, even if that higher average

01:08:36.009 --> 01:08:38.739
does not put you in the range of a diabetic.

01:08:38.739 --> 01:08:44.510
So now the question becomes, are there tools
to help us manage that?

01:08:44.510 --> 01:08:45.640
One tool would just be the A1C.

01:08:45.640 --> 01:08:50.569
You could say, "Well, I'm just going to get
my hemoglobin A1C measured every six months."

01:08:50.569 --> 01:08:56.770
The problem with that, as we know, is that
hemoglobin A1C is very easy to mislead.

01:08:56.770 --> 01:09:00.870
It's very dependent on red blood cell turnover.

01:09:00.870 --> 01:09:07.030
The more rapidly red blood cells turnover,
the more artificially low the hemoglobin A1C

01:09:07.030 --> 01:09:08.159
will be.

01:09:08.159 --> 01:09:14.049
Conversely, the longer the red blood cell
sticks around, the higher, artificially, the

01:09:14.049 --> 01:09:15.910
hemoglobin A1C will be.

01:09:15.910 --> 01:09:23.219
It's for that reason that we like to use CGM
to actually get true measurements of average

01:09:23.219 --> 01:09:24.480
blood glucose.

01:09:24.480 --> 01:09:30.449
When a patient who doesn't have diabetes puts
on a CGM for the first time, they're invariably

01:09:30.449 --> 01:09:32.489
surprised.

01:09:32.489 --> 01:09:34.469
There's this real learning phase that comes.

01:09:34.469 --> 01:09:35.469
You've done this, Tim.

01:09:35.469 --> 01:09:36.469
I know you were doing this.

01:09:36.469 --> 01:09:37.770
Probably 10 years ago, you wore your first
one.

01:09:37.770 --> 01:09:38.770
Everybody goes through this.

01:09:38.770 --> 01:09:41.069
I remember for me, it's been about eight years
since I started.

01:09:41.069 --> 01:09:45.390
At first, you simply cannot believe the things
that drive your blood glucose up.

01:09:45.390 --> 01:09:47.350
It's so simple —
Tim Ferriss: Can you give some examples?

01:09:47.350 --> 01:09:48.350
Dr. Peter Attia: — it sounds dumb.

01:09:48.350 --> 01:09:49.350
Yeah.

01:09:49.350 --> 01:09:50.350
Yeah.

01:09:50.350 --> 01:09:51.350
I remember the first time I had Raisinets.

01:09:51.350 --> 01:09:52.370
It's not like I didn't expect it to go up.

01:09:52.370 --> 01:09:55.880
I didn't expect it to go up like that.

01:09:55.880 --> 01:09:57.640
Exercise.

01:09:57.640 --> 01:09:58.690
Certain forms of exercise.

01:09:58.690 --> 01:10:01.210
By the way, I'm not saying this is pathologic.

01:10:01.210 --> 01:10:06.420
There's nothing wrong when you're exercising
for your glucose to skyrocket if you're doing

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:10.070
tabatas or you're doing HIIT workouts.

01:10:10.070 --> 01:10:13.840
Again, it's not pathological, but it's super
interesting.

01:10:13.840 --> 01:10:18.179
Other things that I think are pathological
is how much your blood glucose goes up if

01:10:18.179 --> 01:10:19.280
you had a poor night of sleep.

01:10:19.280 --> 01:10:20.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, for sure.

01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:24.150
Dr. Peter Attia: If you have a horrible night
of sleep, your insulin sensitivity gets crushed

01:10:24.150 --> 01:10:26.390
the next day, and your glucose levels go up.

01:10:26.390 --> 01:10:27.679
Well, that's actually very interesting.

01:10:27.679 --> 01:10:33.010
Again, maybe you could argue that adds too
much stress to the fire, but it's an insight,

01:10:33.010 --> 01:10:35.530
nevertheless, that can help drive a behavior
change.

01:10:35.530 --> 01:10:41.100
I think in the book, I cover my 10 best insights
from years of CGM in myself and patients.

01:10:41.100 --> 01:10:42.490
It's about different foods.

01:10:42.490 --> 01:10:45.800
Foods high in fiber are going to have one
effect.

01:10:45.800 --> 01:10:51.199
Lean sources of protein versus fatty sources
of protein, all those sorts of things.

01:10:51.199 --> 01:10:56.429
Ultimately, I think CGM becomes a really helpful
tool for compliance.

01:10:56.429 --> 01:10:58.120
It becomes a behavioral tool.

01:10:58.120 --> 01:11:00.250
We tend to gamify it a little bit.

01:11:00.250 --> 01:11:04.949
I know that when I'm wearing CGM, which, I
just happen to be right now, by the way, I'm

01:11:04.949 --> 01:11:08.770
going to think twice before eating something
stupid.

01:11:08.770 --> 01:11:13.550
Again, it's purely just because my personality
likes to gamify things.

01:11:13.550 --> 01:11:19.679
It's like, "Yeah, I'm tempted to go and eat
all the leftover waffles on my kid's plate,

01:11:19.679 --> 01:11:20.679
but I just know."

01:11:20.679 --> 01:11:26.550
It's like, "Eh, it's going to shoot my blood
glucose up, and I like not doing that.

01:11:26.550 --> 01:11:33.820
Where I think people get into trouble and
where the counter-argument is, which is a

01:11:33.820 --> 01:11:39.239
fair argument, is, look, if you only index
on blood glucose, you could still end up doing

01:11:39.239 --> 01:11:41.699
a bunch of things that aren't healthy.

01:11:41.699 --> 01:11:47.239
If you ate bacon for every meal every day,
your blood glucose would not go that high,

01:11:47.239 --> 01:11:50.239
but it's probably not the healthiest choice.

01:11:50.239 --> 01:11:51.920
I would agree with that.

01:11:51.920 --> 01:11:58.570
But by that logic, we shouldn't look at body
weight, either, because by smoking cigarettes,

01:11:58.570 --> 01:12:00.130
your body weight will go down.

01:12:00.130 --> 01:12:03.540
Does that mean body weight is a lousy measurement?

01:12:03.540 --> 01:12:04.540
No.

01:12:04.540 --> 01:12:10.460
It just means that any measurement in isolation
can be ridiculous and can be gamed.

01:12:10.460 --> 01:12:14.989
We shouldn't ignore blood glucose any more
than we should ignore body weight or body

01:12:14.989 --> 01:12:16.760
fat or body composition.

01:12:16.760 --> 01:12:21.100
We just have to understand that it's one of
many tools that we can look at.

01:12:21.100 --> 01:12:24.780
The CGM, again, do I think this is necessary
to live a longer, healthier life?

01:12:24.780 --> 01:12:26.210
Of course not.

01:12:26.210 --> 01:12:32.070
It's simply one tool that we have to help
us understand how to regulate one of the four

01:12:32.070 --> 01:12:33.070
macronutrients.

01:12:33.070 --> 01:12:36.960
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to just add two things
to that.

01:12:36.960 --> 01:12:43.659
The first is people listening who have not
experienced a CGM may hear puncture filaments

01:12:43.659 --> 01:12:48.739
and imagine something out of Hostel 2 or something
like that.

01:12:48.739 --> 01:12:55.540
I will say that 2008 was the first time I
used a Dexcom, which was the size of the cell

01:12:55.540 --> 01:12:57.000
phone used by —
Dr. Peter Attia: It's like a baseball.

01:12:57.000 --> 01:12:58.070
Tim Ferriss: — Gordon Gekko in Wall Street.

01:12:58.070 --> 01:12:59.510
It was huge.

01:12:59.510 --> 01:13:05.179
The experience of putting what felt like,
to me, subjectively barbecue tongs into my

01:13:05.179 --> 01:13:09.070
abdomen was very unpleasant at the time.

01:13:09.070 --> 01:13:15.070
I have, however, tried the latest generations,
and you basically feel nothing.

01:13:15.070 --> 01:13:18.139
I mean, it's so easy in my experience.

01:13:18.139 --> 01:13:23.070
I don't know how many of your patients report
a lot of pain with that, but I was shocked

01:13:23.070 --> 01:13:26.620
by how completely innocuous the whole procedure
was.

01:13:26.620 --> 01:13:28.830
It was very, very mild, very quick, very easy.

01:13:28.830 --> 01:13:32.239
So I just wanted to mention that.

01:13:32.239 --> 01:13:38.701
Secondly, this probably applies more to your
patients than to you, but in the book, you

01:13:38.701 --> 01:13:47.050
mentioned the Hawthorne effect, which I think
was first coined in manufacturing, and looking

01:13:47.050 --> 01:13:51.050
at worker effectiveness or output.

01:13:51.050 --> 01:13:56.540
That is the phenomenon whereby people modify
their behavior when they're being watched.

01:13:56.540 --> 01:14:02.290
I think that is another selling point for
some form of accountability.

01:14:02.290 --> 01:14:04.100
Is there anything you'd like to add to that?

01:14:04.100 --> 01:14:05.100
Dr. Peter Attia: No.

01:14:05.100 --> 01:14:08.800
I mean, I think there are some patients of
ours who actually want to utilize the Hawthorne

01:14:08.800 --> 01:14:09.900
effect.

01:14:09.900 --> 01:14:13.570
If we agree mutually to do so, we can see
all their data.

01:14:13.570 --> 01:14:17.320
They can just say, "Look, I want you to be
looking at my CGM data every day.

01:14:17.320 --> 01:14:22.780
You're an accountability partner for me, so
I'm going to eat less junk food."

01:14:22.780 --> 01:14:31.570
Tim Ferriss: What are some of the other perhaps
under-emphasized or underutilized metrics

01:14:31.570 --> 01:14:32.970
that you think are important?

01:14:32.970 --> 01:14:38.820
This could be in the realm of metrics or tools.

01:14:38.820 --> 01:14:40.800
It could be forms of screening.

01:14:40.800 --> 01:14:44.330
It could be blood markers of different types.

01:14:44.330 --> 01:14:48.750
There are a few that come to mind that I imagine
are on this list.

01:14:48.750 --> 01:14:54.050
It could be physical performance markers of
some type.

01:14:54.050 --> 01:14:55.420
What are some that come to mind?

01:14:55.420 --> 01:15:03.310
Dr. Peter Attia: If we're going to just talk
in magnitude, VO2 max and muscular strength

01:15:03.310 --> 01:15:05.290
stand in a league of their own.

01:15:05.290 --> 01:15:11.030
There's really nothing that's within the ZIP
code of those two metrics.

01:15:11.030 --> 01:15:19.949
Having very, very high VO2 max for your age
and sex, and being very strong, I mean, again,

01:15:19.949 --> 01:15:25.340
they have more of a positive impact than any
single thing we can think of has a negative

01:15:25.340 --> 01:15:26.340
impact.

01:15:26.340 --> 01:15:32.240
That includes having end-stage renal disease,
being on the wait list for a kidney while

01:15:32.240 --> 01:15:36.360
you get dialysis, being a smoker, having high
blood pressure, having type two diabetes,

01:15:36.360 --> 01:15:37.360
being obese.

01:15:37.360 --> 01:15:44.210
The downside of those things is relatively
small compared to the upside of having a high

01:15:44.210 --> 01:15:47.480
VO2 max and being very strong.

01:15:47.480 --> 01:15:52.750
We almost shouldn't even talk about things
until people are willing to accept that fact

01:15:52.750 --> 01:15:55.949
and take the steps necessary to address them.

01:15:55.949 --> 01:16:00.429
Now, you might say, "Well, how many people
can become very, very strong or have a very

01:16:00.429 --> 01:16:01.620
high VO2 max?"

01:16:01.620 --> 01:16:02.870
The answer is I don't know.

01:16:02.870 --> 01:16:06.460
I don't know, especially on the VO2 max side.

01:16:06.460 --> 01:16:12.520
To get into that top 2.5 percent of the population
where you really start to see an enormous

01:16:12.520 --> 01:16:20.949
gap between you and everybody else in terms
of lifespan, yeah, maybe only a quarter of

01:16:20.949 --> 01:16:24.110
the population has the potential to get there.

01:16:24.110 --> 01:16:28.880
But the point is everybody has the potential
to be more fit than they are, outside of people

01:16:28.880 --> 01:16:30.750
who are already doing everything they can.

01:16:30.750 --> 01:16:36.970
So just going from being in the bottom 25
percent of the population to the 25th to 50th

01:16:36.970 --> 01:16:41.880
percentile of the population cuts your risk
of all-cause mortality in half at any point

01:16:41.880 --> 01:16:42.880
in time.

01:16:42.880 --> 01:16:45.780
There's nothing that compares to that.

01:16:45.780 --> 01:16:48.949
There's no drug out there that's going to
do that.

01:16:48.949 --> 01:16:53.530
Again, I think we could talk about drugs all
day long and how I think they're really important

01:16:53.530 --> 01:16:58.949
and how we could think about controlling apoB
and rapamycin and metformin, all these other

01:16:58.949 --> 01:16:59.949
things.

01:16:59.949 --> 01:17:03.970
All that stuff is pixie dust compared to what
these things are.

01:17:03.970 --> 01:17:05.900
On the strength side, the same thing.

01:17:05.900 --> 01:17:09.510
I mean, anyone who's got older parents knows
this.

01:17:09.510 --> 01:17:14.250
If you really want to watch somebody suffer
when they're aging, you watch them losing

01:17:14.250 --> 01:17:17.710
their cognition, and you watch them losing
their physical body.

01:17:17.710 --> 01:17:25.429
You watch them lose the ability to move around
with ease, to have balance, to be pain free,

01:17:25.429 --> 01:17:26.429
all of these things.

01:17:26.429 --> 01:17:30.920
Well, a big part of that is being strong.

01:17:30.920 --> 01:17:36.030
Strength is something we can actually hang
onto as we age.

01:17:36.030 --> 01:17:42.560
We lose quickness long before we lose strength.

01:17:42.560 --> 01:17:43.560
That's the good news.

01:17:43.560 --> 01:17:46.350
The bad news is you have to be very deliberate
in how you train it.

01:17:46.350 --> 01:17:49.980
I'm sure on the podcast, you've discussed
type one and type two muscle fibers.

01:17:49.980 --> 01:17:50.980
People are probably familiar with that.

01:17:50.980 --> 01:17:53.000
Tim Ferriss: Well, why don't you reiterate
that?

01:17:53.000 --> 01:17:56.870
Dr. Peter Attia: Our muscles are made up of
these fibers.

01:17:56.870 --> 01:17:59.210
They're very unique properties.

01:17:59.210 --> 01:18:04.850
Muscles are these multicellular things where
the cells are these long fibers that contract

01:18:04.850 --> 01:18:05.989
past each other.

01:18:05.989 --> 01:18:08.650
That's what creates the contraction of a muscle.

01:18:08.650 --> 01:18:12.170
But if you dig a little bit deeper, you have
different types of fibers.

01:18:12.170 --> 01:18:21.830
You have type one fibers, which are fueled
mostly, if not entirely, by an aerobic process.

01:18:21.830 --> 01:18:23.659
They can use fat.

01:18:23.659 --> 01:18:27.670
They can use glucose, but they do so in the
presence of oxygen primarily.

01:18:27.670 --> 01:18:33.739
They're very slow to fatigue, which is mostly
where the name comes from, but they don't

01:18:33.739 --> 01:18:36.560
generate that much force.

01:18:36.560 --> 01:18:43.090
These are the muscle fibers that are mostly
on display when you're walking or just carrying

01:18:43.090 --> 01:18:44.330
out activities of daily living.

01:18:44.330 --> 01:18:46.750
You can go for a walk for —
Tim Ferriss: Commonly called slow twitch,

01:18:46.750 --> 01:18:47.870
as you alluded to.

01:18:47.870 --> 01:18:48.870
Slow twitch muscle fibers.

01:18:48.870 --> 01:18:49.870
Dr. Peter Attia: Correct.

01:18:49.870 --> 01:18:50.870
Yeah.

01:18:50.870 --> 01:18:53.090
You can do things for a long period of time,
and these things don't fatigue.

01:18:53.090 --> 01:18:58.300
Conversely, another subset of these fibers
are type two fibers.

01:18:58.300 --> 01:19:00.570
They're called fast twitch muscle fibers.

01:19:00.570 --> 01:19:01.940
They're fast to fatigue.

01:19:01.940 --> 01:19:04.110
Now, they're much more powerful.

01:19:04.110 --> 01:19:07.719
When a type two fiber contracts, far more
is happening.

01:19:07.719 --> 01:19:12.870
It's generating far more force, but it's doing
so with a different metabolic strategy.

01:19:12.870 --> 01:19:13.870
It's glycolytic.

01:19:13.870 --> 01:19:20.239
It's just using glucose, broken down glycogen,
and it's going to fatigue quickly.

01:19:20.239 --> 01:19:24.430
It's accumulating metabolic byproducts that
are leading to fatigue.

01:19:24.430 --> 01:19:29.340
There's a reason why, if I say, "Tim, jump
up and down as high as you can, as many times

01:19:29.340 --> 01:19:31.889
as you can," this is not going to be a long
exercise.

01:19:31.889 --> 01:19:35.020
We don't have to sit and wait two hours for
you to finish that.

01:19:35.020 --> 01:19:37.960
You're going to fatigue pretty quickly if
you're doing maximal jumps.

01:19:37.960 --> 01:19:42.830
Whereas, if I say, "Jump rope," you could
do that for 30 minutes easily if you're relatively

01:19:42.830 --> 01:19:43.830
fit person.

01:19:43.830 --> 01:19:45.889
So you get the sense of the difference.

01:19:45.889 --> 01:19:53.450
Now, as we age, we lose power and explosiveness
more than anything else.

01:19:53.450 --> 01:19:54.949
We lose that the soonest.

01:19:54.949 --> 01:19:58.660
That's due to the atrophy of the type two
fiber.

01:19:58.660 --> 01:20:01.560
Again, this comes back down to strategy.

01:20:01.560 --> 01:20:06.300
That's why, if you want to live a longer,
better life, you have to have a strategy in

01:20:06.300 --> 01:20:09.100
place for maintaining type two fibers.

01:20:09.100 --> 01:20:14.350
You're not going to get that without resistance.

01:20:14.350 --> 01:20:17.050
You can't say, "Look, I play tennis every
day.

01:20:17.050 --> 01:20:18.270
I walk every day."

01:20:18.270 --> 01:20:19.270
Those things are great.

01:20:19.270 --> 01:20:21.570
They're not doing something for your type
two fibers.

01:20:21.570 --> 01:20:22.570
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:20:22.570 --> 01:20:23.659
Let me hop in for a second.

01:20:23.659 --> 01:20:32.429
I want to get specific on that because I think
some of the approaches or tools might be counterintuitive

01:20:32.429 --> 01:20:33.429
for folks.

01:20:33.429 --> 01:20:36.139
First, just some random trivia for folks who
might be interested.

01:20:36.139 --> 01:20:41.909
If you've ever carved a chicken or a turkey,
neither of those like to fly very much.

01:20:41.909 --> 01:20:44.639
The breast meat tends to be white, and then
you have the dark meat.

01:20:44.639 --> 01:20:49.219
Well, there you have type two, type one fibers
respectively.

01:20:49.219 --> 01:20:51.550
So just for the next time you're cutting up
the bird.

01:20:51.550 --> 01:20:52.550
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:20:52.550 --> 01:20:56.320
The reason the legs are dark is they have
much more mitochondria.

01:20:56.320 --> 01:21:03.010
Those are their slow twitch muscles there
that are heavily dependent on oxygen delivery

01:21:03.010 --> 01:21:04.710
and oxidative phosphorylation.

01:21:04.710 --> 01:21:08.030
Whereas, the white meat, the breast meat is
very fast twitch.

01:21:08.030 --> 01:21:10.900
Quick, quick, quick flapping.

01:21:10.900 --> 01:21:16.240
Doesn't have the mitochondria, has far less
of it, and therefore that's a less oxygen

01:21:16.240 --> 01:21:17.240
rich muscle.

01:21:17.240 --> 01:21:18.240
Tim Ferriss: I think the quick, quick flap
—

01:21:18.240 --> 01:21:19.240
Dr. Peter Attia: We, of course, look — yeah,
we look all red.

01:21:19.240 --> 01:21:20.240
Tim Ferriss: Go ahead.

01:21:20.240 --> 01:21:21.240
Go ahead.

01:21:21.240 --> 01:21:22.240
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:21:22.240 --> 01:21:23.240
When you dissect us, of course, you don't
see that difference.

01:21:23.240 --> 01:21:24.770
Our type one and type two fibers are less
clearly separated.

01:21:24.770 --> 01:21:32.909
Tim Ferriss: If someone wants to improve strength,
correct me if I'm wrong, but you have a number

01:21:32.909 --> 01:21:35.610
of — maybe they're not just heuristics.

01:21:35.610 --> 01:21:41.630
I mean, they're direct indicators, but you
have a number of things that you focus on.

01:21:41.630 --> 01:21:43.199
I'm sure there are many others.

01:21:43.199 --> 01:21:49.179
But in the strength category, I would love
to know how you define very strong.

01:21:49.179 --> 01:21:55.370
Perhaps this would be a place to talk about,
or at least include, hand strength, farmer's

01:21:55.370 --> 01:22:01.260
carry, or anything else that you'd like to
add that might not immediately show up on

01:22:01.260 --> 01:22:03.639
someone's mental radar when they're thinking
of strength.

01:22:03.639 --> 01:22:04.639
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:22:04.639 --> 01:22:10.100
I mean, there are a handful of strength categories
that we think really matter and just ideas

01:22:10.100 --> 01:22:13.520
that are important when you think about strength.

01:22:13.520 --> 01:22:19.370
One of them is the importance of understanding
eccentric versus concentric strength and how

01:22:19.370 --> 01:22:21.000
both are very important.

01:22:21.000 --> 01:22:26.460
I actually put something up on Instagram the
other day about this, where I just explained

01:22:26.460 --> 01:22:32.500
that the concentric phase is basically the
go and the eccentric phase is the slow.

01:22:32.500 --> 01:22:39.960
You accelerate through concentric force, which
is the force a muscle generates as it is shortening.

01:22:39.960 --> 01:22:41.370
That moves you forward.

01:22:41.370 --> 01:22:47.750
The eccentric phase, which is equally important
but oft ignored, is the strength or the force

01:22:47.750 --> 01:22:51.370
that a muscle is exhibiting as it is lengthening.

01:22:51.370 --> 01:22:52.710
That's what's decelerating.

01:22:52.710 --> 01:22:54.760
That's what's slowing you down.

01:22:54.760 --> 01:23:01.780
Andy Galpin, I think, recently talked about
a great metric, and I agree, this is a fantastic

01:23:01.780 --> 01:23:02.780
metric.

01:23:02.780 --> 01:23:05.489
I'll check mine constantly.

01:23:05.489 --> 01:23:08.510
How far a broad jump can you do?

01:23:08.510 --> 01:23:13.830
I want to make sure that I can do a standing
broad jump that's higher than my height laying

01:23:13.830 --> 01:23:14.830
down.

01:23:14.830 --> 01:23:20.060
If I'm five foot 10, I want to make sure I'm
jumping at least six feet on a broad jump.

01:23:20.060 --> 01:23:22.239
A broad jump's a really interesting test.

01:23:22.239 --> 01:23:27.840
It's a profoundly extreme example of concentric
and eccentric strength in the same movement.

01:23:27.840 --> 01:23:32.220
If you're just standing there and you want
to jump six feet in front of you, that requires

01:23:32.220 --> 01:23:33.969
an enormous explosion.

01:23:33.969 --> 01:23:36.530
That's a very high concentric load.

01:23:36.530 --> 01:23:37.530
But guess what?

01:23:37.530 --> 01:23:41.949
If you want to not break your nose when you
land and destroy your knees, you better be

01:23:41.949 --> 01:23:45.050
able to decelerate and slow yourself down.

01:23:45.050 --> 01:23:48.780
That's an unbelievable eccentric ask.

01:23:48.780 --> 01:23:53.620
While that's a very extreme example, consider
walking up and down stairs.

01:23:53.620 --> 01:23:57.340
Walking up the stairs is very taxing concentrically.

01:23:57.340 --> 01:24:00.620
But where do most people get hurt in life?

01:24:00.620 --> 01:24:02.560
It's actually walking downstairs.

01:24:02.560 --> 01:24:09.540
If you watch, especially as people age, the
difficulty they have in slowing themselves

01:24:09.540 --> 01:24:14.780
down when they're coming downstairs or taking
a step off a curb, this is where people are

01:24:14.780 --> 01:24:16.840
falling and breaking their hips.

01:24:16.840 --> 01:24:22.850
It's far less that they're falling due to
concentric weakness and far more that they're

01:24:22.850 --> 01:24:25.560
falling due to eccentric weakness.

01:24:25.560 --> 01:24:32.760
Therefore, principle number one in our strength
training is always be doing both, not necessarily

01:24:32.760 --> 01:24:38.070
in the same movement, not necessarily focusing
equally on a given day, but everything we

01:24:38.070 --> 01:24:42.530
want to do, we want to make sure that we are
hitting the concentric and the eccentric phase,

01:24:42.530 --> 01:24:44.989
not just the more obvious, which is the concentric.

01:24:44.989 --> 01:24:51.570
Tim Ferriss: Now, does that take the form
of simply accentuating the lowering in the

01:24:51.570 --> 01:24:56.100
case of, say, a trap bar deadlift or something
like that, where you have something like two

01:24:56.100 --> 01:24:58.130
seconds up, four seconds down?

01:24:58.130 --> 01:24:59.469
Or does it take other —
Dr. Peter Attia: Yep.

01:24:59.469 --> 01:25:00.469
Tim Ferriss: — specific forms?

01:25:00.469 --> 01:25:01.469
Dr. Peter Attia: Exactly.

01:25:01.469 --> 01:25:02.469
I mean, there are other ways.

01:25:02.469 --> 01:25:03.469
They're a very specific exercise that you'll
do.

01:25:03.469 --> 01:25:09.570
For example, a Nordic rollout, a Nordic fall
for a hamstring exercise where you're — you

01:25:09.570 --> 01:25:10.570
know what I'm talking about.

01:25:10.570 --> 01:25:13.440
When you're kneeling on a mat and your feet
are held in position and you sort of allow

01:25:13.440 --> 01:25:15.409
yourself to slowly come down?

01:25:15.409 --> 01:25:16.949
That's just purely eccentric.

01:25:16.949 --> 01:25:20.869
Tim Ferriss: Requires quite a bit of starting
strength to do.

01:25:20.869 --> 01:25:24.100
Dr. Peter Attia: Well, truthfully, we wouldn't
have people do that out of the gate.

01:25:24.100 --> 01:25:27.650
You'll do that with assistance to start, because
most people simply don't have the hamstring

01:25:27.650 --> 01:25:28.780
strength to do that.

01:25:28.780 --> 01:25:29.780
Yes.

01:25:29.780 --> 01:25:36.190
It can be accomplished greatly by using slow,
what we call negatives.

01:25:36.190 --> 01:25:38.590
So focusing just as much on the negative is
the positive.

01:25:38.590 --> 01:25:44.860
So for example, one of my favorite exercises
to do are step-ups because one, it's a single

01:25:44.860 --> 01:25:48.400
leg exercise, it's a beautiful hip hinge.

01:25:48.400 --> 01:25:52.070
We can talk about it in some detail in a moment
because hip hinging is another one of the

01:25:52.070 --> 01:26:00.989
big principles and it allows you to do a very
nice isolation of the eccentric on the step-down.

01:26:00.989 --> 01:26:02.530
You really have to be able to control that.

01:26:02.530 --> 01:26:06.909
If you can't control it, you're using too
much resistance, obviously.

01:26:06.909 --> 01:26:09.659
So hip hinging is another big important principle.

01:26:09.659 --> 01:26:12.949
Again, hip hinging, when people think about
that, they think, does that have to be a squat

01:26:12.949 --> 01:26:13.949
or a deadlift?

01:26:13.949 --> 01:26:14.949
No, it doesn't have to be.

01:26:14.949 --> 01:26:17.850
It can be a hip thruster, it can be a lunge,
it can be a step-up.

01:26:17.850 --> 01:26:22.150
I think the step-up might be the single most
important one for people to do because it

01:26:22.150 --> 01:26:23.889
doesn't have any axial loading.

01:26:23.889 --> 01:26:25.580
Tim Ferriss: Can you explain what you mean
by that?

01:26:25.580 --> 01:26:26.580
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:26:26.580 --> 01:26:32.159
When you're doing something like a squat or
a deadlift, there's weight that is basically

01:26:32.159 --> 01:26:35.960
pulling your spine down to your hips.

01:26:35.960 --> 01:26:39.280
The squat, it's because the weight is actually
sitting there and the deadlift, it's because

01:26:39.280 --> 01:26:43.619
the weight is being — the force is being
transferred through your arms there.

01:26:43.619 --> 01:26:49.630
But in either case, you are loading the axis
of your spine and that's fine if you know

01:26:49.630 --> 01:26:51.030
how to do it safely.

01:26:51.030 --> 01:26:56.010
But as you know, having done these things,
that's not just something you can walk in

01:26:56.010 --> 01:26:57.030
off the street and do.

01:26:57.030 --> 01:27:02.300
You really have to be coached how to do that
stuff safely.

01:27:02.300 --> 01:27:07.389
Stepping up onto a block is something that's
much safer to do.

01:27:07.389 --> 01:27:10.409
It's also something that you can do with a
single leg at a time, and therefore you get

01:27:10.409 --> 01:27:13.830
to see what your asymmetries are because we
all have them.

01:27:13.830 --> 01:27:21.639
I am so deep down the rabbit hole of step-ups
that I can't even explain the nuances in oblique

01:27:21.639 --> 01:27:28.430
compression, pelvic angles in the difference
between when my left and right leg do it.

01:27:28.430 --> 01:27:35.490
But tinkering with that stuff allows me to
work out so many kinks with how my body works.

01:27:35.490 --> 01:27:40.300
You mentioned another one earlier, which is
being able to carry heavy things.

01:27:40.300 --> 01:27:44.440
This is just such an essential skill for our
species.

01:27:44.440 --> 01:27:46.489
It's something that we do better than anything.

01:27:46.489 --> 01:27:49.830
There is no animal that can carry with their
hands what we can do.

01:27:49.830 --> 01:27:53.699
Certainly a strong male, but certainly even
a strong female can carry their body weight

01:27:53.699 --> 01:27:54.699
in their hands.

01:27:54.699 --> 01:27:59.940
Half their body weight in each hand is not
an insurmountable ask for us.

01:27:59.940 --> 01:28:01.690
So carrying got so much —
Tim Ferriss: Would that be a hypothetical

01:28:01.690 --> 01:28:03.510
target for your patients?

01:28:03.510 --> 01:28:07.790
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, we would like to see
our patients carry half their body weight

01:28:07.790 --> 01:28:08.790
in each hand for a minute.

01:28:08.790 --> 01:28:09.790
Yeah.

01:28:09.790 --> 01:28:10.790
Tim Ferriss: For a minute?

01:28:10.790 --> 01:28:12.060
Dr. Peter Attia: So walk around with half
your body weight in each hand for a minute

01:28:12.060 --> 01:28:18.710
and having strong hands, it's one of the most
correlated findings with longevity.

01:28:18.710 --> 01:28:21.560
So we talked earlier about what does it mean
to be really, really strong?

01:28:21.560 --> 01:28:26.159
Well, unfortunately, the data on this are
based on what the studies show and the studies

01:28:26.159 --> 01:28:30.290
are testing interesting things, but they're
not exhaustive.

01:28:30.290 --> 01:28:35.800
So they're usually looking at grip strength,
leg extension, bench press are the most commonly

01:28:35.800 --> 01:28:41.550
tested things, but grip strength comes up
over and over and over again in studies as

01:28:41.550 --> 01:28:45.740
such a proxy for longevity, lower risk.

01:28:45.740 --> 01:28:51.970
I think there's a figure in my book that talks
about the unbelievable monotonic decline in

01:28:51.970 --> 01:28:58.070
both the risk of onset of dementia and death
from dementia as grip strength increases.

01:28:58.070 --> 01:29:02.290
Again, you just kind of go back to the Bradford
Hill criteria and you look at the strength

01:29:02.290 --> 01:29:07.600
of the associations, you look at the consistency
of these associations, you look at the dose

01:29:07.600 --> 01:29:09.409
effect of these associations.

01:29:09.409 --> 01:29:13.300
Very hard for me to believe that there isn't
causal relationships here.

01:29:13.300 --> 01:29:18.389
That being stronger training to be stronger
will actually improve outcomes.

01:29:18.389 --> 01:29:24.010
Not just that strength is a marker of health,
which is obviously correlated with living

01:29:24.010 --> 01:29:25.139
longer, if that makes sense.

01:29:25.139 --> 01:29:27.480
I mean there's a distinction there, so.

01:29:27.480 --> 01:29:33.060
Yeah, we think that carrying things is very
important and yeah, I'll go through different

01:29:33.060 --> 01:29:34.060
phases.

01:29:34.060 --> 01:29:35.060
As you know, I love rucking.

01:29:35.060 --> 01:29:39.330
So that's another thing that I think is just
a great all round way to now combine two different

01:29:39.330 --> 01:29:42.690
types of exercise, something that's part of
strength and something that's part of endurance.

01:29:42.690 --> 01:29:45.190
Also, rucking gives you — so just for people
who don't know, rucking is just basically

01:29:45.190 --> 01:29:48.270
carrying a really heavy weighted backpack.

01:29:48.270 --> 01:29:54.159
Probably one of the most important tools used
in the training of special forces in the military.

01:29:54.159 --> 01:29:59.990
We have so many friends that have been through
that and I'm amazed at how much they rucked.

01:29:59.990 --> 01:30:02.220
It's like eight hours a day.

01:30:02.220 --> 01:30:05.409
They're just walking around with 75 pounds
on their back if not more.

01:30:05.409 --> 01:30:08.909
Tim Ferriss: What does your protocol methodology
look like for rucking?

01:30:08.909 --> 01:30:16.239
Dr. Peter Attia: So I use 55 to 60 pounds,
and I'll typically do an hour ruck because

01:30:16.239 --> 01:30:17.239
of where I live.

01:30:17.239 --> 01:30:18.969
It's really hilly, so I really like that.

01:30:18.969 --> 01:30:22.300
But going up the hills, I'm going hard.

01:30:22.300 --> 01:30:25.969
I'm really pushing my cardio up the hills
and I'm trying to find the steepest hills

01:30:25.969 --> 01:30:31.460
possible to come down because that's working
that huge eccentric, that gear, it's really

01:30:31.460 --> 01:30:35.670
forcing me to be able to decelerate on the
way down those hills with a lot of weight.

01:30:35.670 --> 01:30:36.790
You're not getting the pounding.

01:30:36.790 --> 01:30:38.050
I think that's the beauty of —
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

01:30:38.050 --> 01:30:41.270
Dr. Peter Attia: You're not pounding your
knee with the impact you would if you had

01:30:41.270 --> 01:30:42.969
to run to produce that effect.

01:30:42.969 --> 01:30:48.050
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's probably the most
compelling reason that I started rucking a

01:30:48.050 --> 01:30:55.429
few years ago, especially in preparation for
certain alpine hunts and so on, did a ton

01:30:55.429 --> 01:31:00.780
of rucking and the collateral benefits, they're
not really collateral.

01:31:00.780 --> 01:31:05.610
The direct benefits were this sort of holy
shit effect that sometimes I've seen with

01:31:05.610 --> 01:31:09.890
kettlebell swings and other things where this
translation that you wouldn't expect pops

01:31:09.890 --> 01:31:12.240
up in all sorts of ways.

01:31:12.240 --> 01:31:15.719
I used to be a runner, but I ended up — you
probably didn't know this, but I used to be

01:31:15.719 --> 01:31:19.810
a cross country runner when I was, say, up
to about age 15.

01:31:19.810 --> 01:31:26.239
Then through poor choices, like doing lots
of wrestling and other impact sports, developed

01:31:26.239 --> 01:31:28.179
knee issues and so stopped running.

01:31:28.179 --> 01:31:35.810
But I always missed the sensation of getting
a good workout while walking or moving in

01:31:35.810 --> 01:31:38.130
some fashion on ground.

01:31:38.130 --> 01:31:39.489
Rucking solved that.

01:31:39.489 --> 01:31:46.170
So coming back to VO2 max, so VO2 max, I've
always wondered to what degree someone can

01:31:46.170 --> 01:31:51.750
improve VO2 max, you already spoke to the
benefits, even if you're moving from say,

01:31:51.750 --> 01:31:55.090
25th percentile, the 50th percentile, huge
benefit, right?

01:31:55.090 --> 01:31:57.980
I mean half I think you said halving, halfing
cutting in half certain mortality risks.

01:31:57.980 --> 01:32:01.980
What have you found to be the best approaches
for improving VO2 max?

01:32:01.980 --> 01:32:05.680
Dr. Peter Attia: Well, first off, it can actually
be improved quite a bit.

01:32:05.680 --> 01:32:11.070
I think the research out there understates
how much it can be approved because the research

01:32:11.070 --> 01:32:12.860
studies are relatively short.

01:32:12.860 --> 01:32:18.180
So it's true that in a 12-week study, you
might see an eight percent improvement or

01:32:18.180 --> 01:32:20.200
a 10 percent improvement.

01:32:20.200 --> 01:32:24.750
I would say that's just the tip of the iceberg,
we're not talking about 12 weeks of training

01:32:24.750 --> 01:32:25.750
here.

01:32:25.750 --> 01:32:30.909
We're talking about a lifetime of training
and we're talking about 25-50 percent improvement

01:32:30.909 --> 01:32:35.449
in VO2 max that anybody who's been involved
in serious training will see all day long.

01:32:35.449 --> 01:32:40.300
So the, how is also very important, the analogy
I like to use, which I'm borrowing from one

01:32:40.300 --> 01:32:46.290
of my old cycling coaches, is that think about
building a pyramid.

01:32:46.290 --> 01:32:49.530
The VO2 max is the height of the pyramid.

01:32:49.530 --> 01:32:57.540
So if you want to build a really high pyramid,
it also has to have a really wide base.

01:32:57.540 --> 01:33:04.600
So the key for building a high VO2 max is
saying, "I'm going to spend about 80 percent

01:33:04.600 --> 01:33:08.060
of my aerobic training time in zone two."

01:33:08.060 --> 01:33:09.340
This is counterintuitive.

01:33:09.340 --> 01:33:11.619
A lot of people think, "Oh, my god.

01:33:11.619 --> 01:33:14.260
If I want to build a high VO2 max, it has
to be all intense training."

01:33:14.260 --> 01:33:20.050
No, you have to first and foremost build that
huge aerobic base.

01:33:20.050 --> 01:33:21.050
That is the pyramid.

01:33:21.050 --> 01:33:22.050
Tim Ferriss: That foundation.

01:33:22.050 --> 01:33:23.620
Dr. Peter Attia: That allows you to make the
peak higher.

01:33:23.620 --> 01:33:30.489
So once you have that aerobic base, that other
20 percent of the time is used well above.

01:33:30.489 --> 01:33:35.730
So basically most people are training too
hard to build the base, but not hard enough

01:33:35.730 --> 01:33:36.730
to build the peak.

01:33:36.730 --> 01:33:41.219
So I kind of think of as garbage training
zone and not to rip on —

01:33:41.219 --> 01:33:43.690
Tim Ferriss: Not to get too technical.

01:33:43.690 --> 01:33:45.100
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:33:45.100 --> 01:33:49.320
But that's sort of the problem with doing
a lot of fun classes and stuff.

01:33:49.320 --> 01:33:54.949
Look, it's absolutely better than sitting
on the couch for sure, but it's not specific

01:33:54.949 --> 01:33:56.790
enough to achieve this goal.

01:33:56.790 --> 01:34:00.429
So you really want to separate that zone two
from that zone five.

01:34:00.429 --> 01:34:06.780
When it comes to VO2 max, you basically have
to be in the three- to eight-minute range

01:34:06.780 --> 01:34:07.780
is the sweet spot.

01:34:07.780 --> 01:34:11.440
So it's got to be intervals that are about
three to eight minutes.

01:34:11.440 --> 01:34:15.619
So three minutes at the low end, meaning you're
doing something that is so hard, you can only

01:34:15.619 --> 01:34:18.119
do it for about three minutes and at the high
end, eight minutes.

01:34:18.119 --> 01:34:21.429
So if you can do it for more than eight minutes,
it's not doing a lot for your VO2 max.

01:34:21.429 --> 01:34:25.450
If you can't do it for three minutes, it's
also not doing the maximum amount for your

01:34:25.450 --> 01:34:26.450
VO2 max.

01:34:26.450 --> 01:34:29.851
Tim Ferriss: Just for clarity, that's the
zone five that you're referring to, building

01:34:29.851 --> 01:34:30.851
the peak?

01:34:30.851 --> 01:34:31.851
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:34:31.851 --> 01:34:32.851
Yeah, exactly.

01:34:32.851 --> 01:34:36.719
Here you're typically doing about a one-to-one
work to rest recovery.

01:34:36.719 --> 01:34:38.690
That's how hard these intervals are.

01:34:38.690 --> 01:34:43.230
So if you do three minutes at that, whatever
that output is, you're going to need about

01:34:43.230 --> 01:34:47.519
three minutes of recovery before you can do
it again.

01:34:47.519 --> 01:34:54.380
So we typically say, "Look, a good starting
point is four by four," four on four off four

01:34:54.380 --> 01:34:55.770
times.

01:34:55.770 --> 01:34:59.360
That's just a great workout to start just
doing that workout once a week.

01:34:59.360 --> 01:35:02.820
So let's say three days a week you're going
to do your cardio and it's going to be kind

01:35:02.820 --> 01:35:05.350
of the low-end zone two cardio.

01:35:05.350 --> 01:35:11.139
One day a week, you pick an exertion level
that you can barely get through four minutes

01:35:11.139 --> 01:35:16.230
of it, you can do it, but you've a little
bit left in the tank, but not much.

01:35:16.230 --> 01:35:18.500
That you need four minutes to recover.

01:35:18.500 --> 01:35:20.370
Then you do that four times.

01:35:20.370 --> 01:35:22.900
Sandwich between a warmup and a cool down.

01:35:22.900 --> 01:35:25.869
Tim Ferriss: If I'm understanding correctly,
that would be the building of the peak.

01:35:25.869 --> 01:35:28.520
What does the zone two training look like?

01:35:28.520 --> 01:35:31.530
Dr. Peter Attia: Long and slow.

01:35:31.530 --> 01:35:32.530
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:35:32.530 --> 01:35:33.530
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:35:33.530 --> 01:35:42.679
Tim Ferriss: Aside from subjective perceived
exertion, is there a heart rate range or anything

01:35:42.679 --> 01:35:45.350
else that you can use as sort of a proxy indicator?

01:35:45.350 --> 01:35:48.380
So people can have something to aim for?

01:35:48.380 --> 01:35:51.970
Dr. Peter Attia: Once you get fit enough,
lactate becomes really good.

01:35:51.970 --> 01:35:57.800
So measuring lactate with a point of care
like a finger prick device is what I do.

01:35:57.800 --> 01:36:03.080
You're looking for a lactate level of about
1.7 to two millimole for most people is the

01:36:03.080 --> 01:36:04.650
zone.

01:36:04.650 --> 01:36:09.310
For people who aren't yet fit enough, their
lactates are usually going to be way higher

01:36:09.310 --> 01:36:12.889
than that while they're still training that
energy system.

01:36:12.889 --> 01:36:14.630
Or if people just don't want to do that.

01:36:14.630 --> 01:36:20.320
For those people RPE, rate of perceived exertion,
is really the best way to do it and it's far

01:36:20.320 --> 01:36:22.510
more accurate than heart rate.

01:36:22.510 --> 01:36:28.580
So the RPE is you should be able to talk,
but you don't want to.

01:36:28.580 --> 01:36:31.450
So today I did a zone two ride.

01:36:31.450 --> 01:36:35.910
I spent the entire — this is very unusual,
but just because of the way it was today,

01:36:35.910 --> 01:36:39.020
I spent the entire zone two on a phone call.

01:36:39.020 --> 01:36:41.850
My entire workout was on a phone call.

01:36:41.850 --> 01:36:46.280
There was absolutely no confusion on the guy
on the other end of that line that I was on

01:36:46.280 --> 01:36:53.510
my bike and that I wanted him to talk more
than me, like I was breathing like this.

01:36:53.510 --> 01:36:55.400
He was like, okay.

01:36:55.400 --> 01:37:00.810
So yeah, I mean, that's kind of how I was
going at it.

01:37:00.810 --> 01:37:05.430
I can nasally breathe when I'm doing zone
two, but I'm right at the limits of what I

01:37:05.430 --> 01:37:06.430
can do.

01:37:06.430 --> 01:37:07.940
It's very easy for me to nasally breathe.

01:37:07.940 --> 01:37:09.680
I don't mouth breathe at night or anything
like that.

01:37:09.680 --> 01:37:11.610
So that kind of gives you a sense of it.

01:37:11.610 --> 01:37:13.840
If you can talk pretty easily while you're
doing it.

01:37:13.840 --> 01:37:17.600
If you're out on a walk and you're talking
to somebody, that's too easy.

01:37:17.600 --> 01:37:22.390
If you can't talk at all, that's too hard.

01:37:22.390 --> 01:37:25.440
I think that, by far the best test.

01:37:25.440 --> 01:37:27.870
Tim Ferriss: Just to give people some more
specifics.

01:37:27.870 --> 01:37:31.440
So for the zone two, how long would a target
session be?

01:37:31.440 --> 01:37:33.159
How many times per week?

01:37:33.159 --> 01:37:39.040
Then similarly for the four-by-four, let's
just say that you described for the zone five

01:37:39.040 --> 01:37:41.670
building the peak, how many times per week
would you do that?

01:37:41.670 --> 01:37:44.440
Dr. Peter Attia: I think if we can do that
once a week is great.

01:37:44.440 --> 01:37:45.440
Tim Ferriss: That's zone five?

01:37:45.440 --> 01:37:47.090
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, zone five.

01:37:47.090 --> 01:37:51.239
I think for zone two, we think minimum effective
dose.

01:37:51.239 --> 01:37:53.030
So again, it depends on where you're starting.

01:37:53.030 --> 01:37:58.500
If you're starting with you've never exercised
in your life, I mean, boy, we'd be happy to

01:37:58.500 --> 01:38:02.909
get you doing two 30-minute a week zone two
sessions, but you very quickly will start

01:38:02.909 --> 01:38:03.969
to adapt to that.

01:38:03.969 --> 01:38:09.400
Then I think we start to get into three hours
being the MED.

01:38:09.400 --> 01:38:13.620
That might be three 60-minute sessions or
four 45-minute sessions.

01:38:13.620 --> 01:38:19.590
Tim Ferriss: Have you ever tried, Peter, and
I'm just going to bring this up because we've

01:38:19.590 --> 01:38:23.860
spent a good amount of time together, and
this has been a bit of a revelation for me.

01:38:23.860 --> 01:38:29.060
Have you ever tried skinning on skis going
uphill with skins on skis?

01:38:29.060 --> 01:38:34.090
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, so Lance Armstrong's
a good buddy and he moved out of Austin to

01:38:34.090 --> 01:38:38.730
Aspen a few years ago, and I keep giving him
crap for it.

01:38:38.730 --> 01:38:39.800
I'm like, "Dude, why did you leave?

01:38:39.800 --> 01:38:40.800
Why leave?"

01:38:40.800 --> 01:38:42.000
He's like, "Yeah, it's tough."

01:38:42.000 --> 01:38:46.960
Especially in the winter because he's a summer
sport guy, but this is his form of winter

01:38:46.960 --> 01:38:47.960
exercise.

01:38:47.960 --> 01:38:50.550
Having him describe it to me, I'm like, "Yeah,
I can see that.

01:38:50.550 --> 01:38:51.970
That sounds pretty awesome, actually."

01:38:51.970 --> 01:39:00.429
Tim Ferriss: So this, Peter, but people listening
may not, I historically am about as far from

01:39:00.429 --> 01:39:05.790
an endurance athlete as you could possibly
be in the sense that if you look at my sports

01:39:05.790 --> 01:39:11.760
career, such as it was, it's all optimizing
for trying to win very quickly within three

01:39:11.760 --> 01:39:15.960
to five minutes generally.

01:39:15.960 --> 01:39:23.880
Anything close to extended exercise has always
been rightly and probably wrongly punishment

01:39:23.880 --> 01:39:24.880
in my view.

01:39:24.880 --> 01:39:29.430
I just have really disliked it, which means
I've gravitated towards weight training, things

01:39:29.430 --> 01:39:30.430
like that.

01:39:30.430 --> 01:39:32.800
Skinning and rucking are the two things that
have changed that.

01:39:32.800 --> 01:39:41.179
My body, including my low back pain and so
on, feels so much better when I am doing skinning

01:39:41.179 --> 01:39:42.179
and rucking.

01:39:42.179 --> 01:39:47.510
I will say with the rucking, and I don't know
what your setup looks like, but I really benefit

01:39:47.510 --> 01:39:49.150
from having a waist strap.

01:39:49.150 --> 01:39:53.240
There are rucking backpacks out there that
do not have a waist strap, and I find those

01:39:53.240 --> 01:40:00.230
can cause some back pain if I have either
too much weight, use them for too long or

01:40:00.230 --> 01:40:02.739
both, but —
Dr. Peter Attia: Well, can I make a shameless

01:40:02.739 --> 01:40:05.860
plug for my friend Jason McCarthy's company?

01:40:05.860 --> 01:40:06.860
GORUCK?

01:40:06.860 --> 01:40:07.860
Tim Ferriss: Please.

01:40:07.860 --> 01:40:08.860
Yeah, please.

01:40:08.860 --> 01:40:10.400
Dr. Peter Attia: I have no affiliation, but
I love Jason and I love the company.

01:40:10.400 --> 01:40:13.020
So yeah, the GORUCK is the way to go here.

01:40:13.020 --> 01:40:18.030
Again, you just need a backpack with weight,
but if you want to invest in it, they make

01:40:18.030 --> 01:40:20.040
amazing packs.

01:40:20.040 --> 01:40:24.440
You can buy these bolt on waistbands that
allow you to support because I'm like you,

01:40:24.440 --> 01:40:29.350
I like to have 80 percent of the weight on
my hips and virtually none of it on my shoulders.

01:40:29.350 --> 01:40:34.290
I don't even link up the little strap across
my chest because I want to be able to breathe

01:40:34.290 --> 01:40:35.290
fully.

01:40:35.290 --> 01:40:41.550
So I'm very light on the shoulders, incredibly
heavy on the hips and their packs and the

01:40:41.550 --> 01:40:42.550
weights that come with them.

01:40:42.550 --> 01:40:44.679
Just make this a very elegant solution.

01:40:44.679 --> 01:40:45.679
Yeah, totally agree.

01:40:45.679 --> 01:40:46.679
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, Yeah.

01:40:46.679 --> 01:40:47.679
So GORUCK.

01:40:47.679 --> 01:40:53.320
I also use GORUCK, so I'll give them a plug,
I don't know the founders, but ended up honing

01:40:53.320 --> 01:40:55.110
in on GORUCK.

01:40:55.110 --> 01:41:01.409
I will say also the breathing component, aside
from the loading distribution that you just

01:41:01.409 --> 01:41:05.810
men mentioned, one of the benefits that I
find of rucking versus, say, a weighted vest,

01:41:05.810 --> 01:41:11.380
which are quite popular in the CrossFit communities
and so on, is where you are bearing the weight

01:41:11.380 --> 01:41:13.159
and also the ability to breathe.

01:41:13.159 --> 01:41:14.550
So those would be two advantages.

01:41:14.550 --> 01:41:17.730
I'm sure there's a lot to be said for the
weighted vests as well.

01:41:17.730 --> 01:41:21.150
Although funny, I'll tell you, Peter, I don't
know if I've ever told you this.

01:41:21.150 --> 01:41:26.560
This was probably 2009, 2010, I decided I
was going to try weighted vests and I was

01:41:26.560 --> 01:41:27.869
like, "This is going to be fantastic.

01:41:27.869 --> 01:41:30.580
I can't wait."

01:41:30.580 --> 01:41:34.730
So I bought this monster, which was loaded
up, it looked like a suicide bomber vest,

01:41:34.730 --> 01:41:38.389
you just had all these — they looked like
canisters full of weight.

01:41:38.389 --> 01:41:39.699
Dr. Peter Attia: How much did they weigh?

01:41:39.699 --> 01:41:41.380
Tim Ferriss: Well, okay, I'll get there.

01:41:41.380 --> 01:41:44.060
So I thought to myself, it's just walking.

01:41:44.060 --> 01:41:48.000
At that point, I was very seriously, and I
know this is not winning any records, but

01:41:48.000 --> 01:41:49.000
for me, it was a lot.

01:41:49.000 --> 01:41:56.780
I was probably 180 and I was deadlifting 475
for reps, which is as high as I got.

01:41:56.780 --> 01:42:01.909
I thought to myself, I walk all the time,
let me get — I think it was a hundred pound

01:42:01.909 --> 01:42:02.909
vest.

01:42:02.909 --> 01:42:07.849
So I get this hundred-pound vest and I put
it on and I decide to go for a leisurely two

01:42:07.849 --> 01:42:14.260
or three-mile walk and I get a mile out and
I'm like, "I can't carry this thing, I'm just

01:42:14.260 --> 01:42:17.500
going to collapse into a puddle on the sidewalk."

01:42:17.500 --> 01:42:23.110
So I had to leave the vest on the sidewalk,
this is in The Mission in San Francisco and

01:42:23.110 --> 01:42:26.030
go back to my house to get a car to come get
it.

01:42:26.030 --> 01:42:30.719
I thought to myself, if anybody steals this
thing, they deserve to have it.

01:42:30.719 --> 01:42:33.050
Not surprisingly, the weight vest did not
move.

01:42:33.050 --> 01:42:34.719
It stayed exactly where it was.

01:42:34.719 --> 01:42:38.550
So yeah, rucking, I suggest rucking and start
later than —

01:42:38.550 --> 01:42:43.900
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, I recommend people
start with a sixth to a quarter of their body

01:42:43.900 --> 01:42:44.900
weight.

01:42:44.900 --> 01:42:45.900
Tim Ferriss: You'll end up dropping.

01:42:45.900 --> 01:42:46.900
It's a good chance you'll end up dropping
it somewhere.

01:42:46.900 --> 01:42:51.540
Dr. Peter Attia: Truthfully, I think Michael
Easter, who's written about this extensively

01:42:51.540 --> 01:42:56.090
in his book The Comfort Crisis, which if your
listeners haven't read that, not that we're

01:42:56.090 --> 01:42:59.750
trying to give them more books to read, but
The Comfort Crisis, I can't recommend highly

01:42:59.750 --> 01:43:00.780
enough.

01:43:00.780 --> 01:43:04.710
I think he said that even the military right
now thinks that you don't really need to go

01:43:04.710 --> 01:43:06.650
above a third of your body weight.

01:43:06.650 --> 01:43:09.890
So let gravity help you more than that.

01:43:09.890 --> 01:43:14.050
But sort of anywhere from a sixth to a third,
your body weight is probably the sweet spot.

01:43:14.050 --> 01:43:18.599
Tim Ferriss: Where I've ended up, and this
will make me sound weak maybe, but is right

01:43:18.599 --> 01:43:19.980
around 45.

01:43:19.980 --> 01:43:25.639
In some cases I've added a bit more weight,
but I probably weigh 175 right now.

01:43:25.639 --> 01:43:32.060
45, and then focusing on inclines, basically
just going for the sort of geographical altitude,

01:43:32.060 --> 01:43:37.650
or not even necessarily altitude, but the
incline challenge rather than slapping on

01:43:37.650 --> 01:43:40.050
a ton of weight and walking on flatter ground.

01:43:40.050 --> 01:43:45.489
What are some other for you, crux — we've
spoken about strength, we've spoken about

01:43:45.489 --> 01:43:54.070
VO2 max, just so I can maybe explicitly explain
why I've spent, and we have spent some time

01:43:54.070 --> 01:44:00.280
on this, it's because these are not purely
— the implications are not purely muscular.

01:44:00.280 --> 01:44:08.139
Maybe this is a place to talk about what you
have learned about neurodegenerative disease,

01:44:08.139 --> 01:44:11.449
and specifically, perhaps, Alzheimer's.

01:44:11.449 --> 01:44:17.599
Because I think this will all tie in and show
people that the sort of Cartesian duality

01:44:17.599 --> 01:44:22.371
of body and mind is really illusory, could
you speak to that?

01:44:22.371 --> 01:44:26.349
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, I think that probably
the first time I took a hard look at this

01:44:26.349 --> 01:44:30.670
would've been 2014, maybe 2015.

01:44:30.670 --> 01:44:38.850
At the time, I had one analyst, his name was
Dan Palenchar, look at this in detail.

01:44:38.850 --> 01:44:44.520
God, it was probably a six to nine-month project.

01:44:44.520 --> 01:44:53.500
it was a really, really exhaustive look at
the literature and the deepest dive we could

01:44:53.500 --> 01:44:58.450
do at the time of what do we know about the
prevention of Alzheimer's disease?

01:44:58.450 --> 01:45:04.360
So again, we started with a very bold hypothesis,
which we were not alone in, but most people

01:45:04.360 --> 01:45:11.070
thought was crazy, which was that these diseases
have some degree of prevention.

01:45:11.070 --> 01:45:17.210
There is a way to prevent these to some extent,
maybe not completely, but luck is not the

01:45:17.210 --> 01:45:22.400
only thing, or bad luck is not the only thing
that is driving these diseases.

01:45:22.400 --> 01:45:23.600
We have some control.

01:45:23.600 --> 01:45:29.830
So to make a very long story short, after
Dan kind of went through all of this analysis

01:45:29.830 --> 01:45:36.460
and after analysis after analysis, the thing
that stood out above everything else was the

01:45:36.460 --> 01:45:39.390
benefit of exercise.

01:45:39.390 --> 01:45:42.219
I just sort of told Dan to go back to the
drawing board.

01:45:42.219 --> 01:45:45.929
I was kind of like, "Dan, I think you screwed
this analysis up, buddy.

01:45:45.929 --> 01:45:51.679
There's zero chance that exercise is actually
the best thing here.

01:45:51.679 --> 01:45:55.530
Are you going to now tell me that chicken
soup is the best thing for colds?

01:45:55.530 --> 01:45:57.690
I wanted some insight here.

01:45:57.690 --> 01:46:03.150
I didn't want some stupid platitude," but
it turned out Dan was right and I was wrong.

01:46:03.150 --> 01:46:06.030
So I think the data are pretty unambiguous.

01:46:06.030 --> 01:46:08.580
In fact, I'm doing a podcast on this.

01:46:08.580 --> 01:46:12.150
I don't know, by the time this one's out,
it might be out.

01:46:12.150 --> 01:46:13.150
I don't know, I don't know.

01:46:13.150 --> 01:46:20.270
But I've got a podcast coming out on brain
health and here we are back again now with

01:46:20.270 --> 01:46:24.540
many more analysts and much more tools at
our disposal to understand the literature

01:46:24.540 --> 01:46:32.949
and still exercise remains the most important
modifiable behavior we have to reduce the

01:46:32.949 --> 01:46:37.950
risk of Alzheimer's disease and probably Parkinson's
and Lewy body dementia, by the way.

01:46:37.950 --> 01:46:42.659
So I can't say that for every single neurodegenerative
disease, I don't have any real insight into

01:46:42.659 --> 01:46:47.570
how you prevent Lou Gehrig's disease, which
is an absolutely horrific disease that is

01:46:47.570 --> 01:46:49.020
fortunately much more rare.

01:46:49.020 --> 01:46:55.869
But when it comes to the big three — Alzheimer's,
Parkinson's, Lewy body dementia — exercise,

01:46:55.869 --> 01:47:01.139
exercise and exercise matter, and part of
that has to do with movement reserve.

01:47:01.139 --> 01:47:05.199
So when you think about Lewy body and Parkinson's,
these are primarily movement disorders.

01:47:05.199 --> 01:47:06.670
So they're kind of on a spectrum.

01:47:06.670 --> 01:47:11.119
So you've got Parkinson's at one end, pure
movement disorder, a little bit of cognitive.

01:47:11.119 --> 01:47:12.489
Lewy body kind of bit of both.

01:47:12.489 --> 01:47:17.429
Then Alzheimer's, much more cognitive, having
a higher movement reserve and having a higher

01:47:17.429 --> 01:47:19.270
cognitive reserve are protective.

01:47:19.270 --> 01:47:21.239
So people —
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by movement

01:47:21.239 --> 01:47:22.239
reserve?

01:47:22.239 --> 01:47:24.320
Just greater range of motion ability?

01:47:24.320 --> 01:47:26.889
So spectrum of movement patterns that they
can sustain?

01:47:26.889 --> 01:47:27.889
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:47:27.889 --> 01:47:29.540
The greater physical capacity.

01:47:29.540 --> 01:47:37.250
So people who are really good dancers, who
are really good at doing complicated, coordinated,

01:47:37.250 --> 01:47:41.969
problem-solving things with their body who
have kinesthetic awareness, they're going

01:47:41.969 --> 01:47:48.599
to have a much slower decline even when diagnosed
with Parkinson's disease, for example.

01:47:48.599 --> 01:47:52.349
So we want our cognitive reserve high, we
want our movement reserve high.

01:47:52.349 --> 01:47:59.710
Then on the other side of that, you get the
sort of hormonal vascular metabolic benefits

01:47:59.710 --> 01:48:02.280
that come from the actual exercise.

01:48:02.280 --> 01:48:07.099
So one is the benefits you get from being
in the state, one is the benefits you get

01:48:07.099 --> 01:48:08.300
accruing the state.

01:48:08.300 --> 01:48:13.080
Tim Ferriss: Could you expand a bit on the
hormonal effects and otherwise, I mean, I

01:48:13.080 --> 01:48:18.400
think this is probably just because it has
words in the acronym that I like, but brain-derived

01:48:18.400 --> 01:48:20.230
neurotrophic factor, et cetera?

01:48:20.230 --> 01:48:23.060
To what extent can you weight some of these
factors?

01:48:23.060 --> 01:48:24.060
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah.

01:48:24.060 --> 01:48:25.060
BDNF is one of the most important.

01:48:25.060 --> 01:48:27.619
Yeah, I don't know I that I could tell you
a relative weighting, but certainly BDNF is

01:48:27.619 --> 01:48:28.810
a very important one.

01:48:28.810 --> 01:48:32.940
So it's a very important growth factor for
neurons.

01:48:32.940 --> 01:48:37.410
But also, of course, when you think about
the metabolic effects and the hormonal effects

01:48:37.410 --> 01:48:41.160
that come from what is exercise doing to cortisol
levels?

01:48:41.160 --> 01:48:44.469
What is exercise doing with glucose and insulin
levels?

01:48:44.469 --> 01:48:49.480
I mean, we see an unbelievably strong association
between type two diabetes and Alzheimer's

01:48:49.480 --> 01:48:50.480
disease.

01:48:50.480 --> 01:48:55.389
Well, again, here's an example of where Medicine
3.0 I think gives you a little bit more confidence

01:48:55.389 --> 01:49:00.570
to make the extension, which is, look, if
type two diabetes is bad, having insulin resistance

01:49:00.570 --> 01:49:04.230
without type two diabetes is also probably
bad.

01:49:04.230 --> 01:49:11.369
Therefore, we want to take all steps possible
to maximize insulin sensitivity, glucose disposal,

01:49:11.369 --> 01:49:15.300
all of these things that fit under the bucket
of metabolic health.

01:49:15.300 --> 01:49:20.540
Again, exercise is not unique in its ability
to act on those things.

01:49:20.540 --> 01:49:25.260
Nutrition absolutely does as well, but boy,
does exercise have a profound effect on those

01:49:25.260 --> 01:49:26.260
things.

01:49:26.260 --> 01:49:28.449
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:49:28.449 --> 01:49:31.280
Let's talk about training the trainer, maybe.

01:49:31.280 --> 01:49:37.030
By that, I mean the vast majority of people
listening to this will not be able to work

01:49:37.030 --> 01:49:41.440
with you or a practice like yours.

01:49:41.440 --> 01:49:46.679
What types of requests can they make of their
physicians?

01:49:46.679 --> 01:49:50.409
What types of tests might they ask for?

01:49:50.409 --> 01:49:59.710
This may be a clumsily worded question, but
how can they make their doctor and their health

01:49:59.710 --> 01:50:01.699
plan per se better?

01:50:01.699 --> 01:50:04.580
Are there any recommendations you have?

01:50:04.580 --> 01:50:08.640
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, look, I think that
it kind of comes back a little bit to your

01:50:08.640 --> 01:50:14.290
medical literacy question at the outset, which
is, I think part of it is also just being

01:50:14.290 --> 01:50:19.650
a bit more clear about what your objectives
are when you're finding a doctor and understanding

01:50:19.650 --> 01:50:21.580
a little bit about what their style is.

01:50:21.580 --> 01:50:24.889
I mean, asking them what is their philosophy
on something?

01:50:24.889 --> 01:50:29.130
How much time do you spend learning about
things that you did not learn in medical school?

01:50:29.130 --> 01:50:37.400
I go back to something I said earlier, which
was, there's almost nothing I do today, Tim,

01:50:37.400 --> 01:50:38.960
that I learned in medical school.

01:50:38.960 --> 01:50:42.139
I can't think of one thing.

01:50:42.139 --> 01:50:46.780
It's not because what I learned in medical
school wasn't valuable.

01:50:46.780 --> 01:50:50.889
Maybe that's the wrong example, by the way,
let's be a little more less glib.

01:50:50.889 --> 01:50:56.579
If I look at the physicians in my practice
who trained in internal medicine and ask the

01:50:56.579 --> 01:51:01.510
question, "What did you learn in residency
that you're using today?"

01:51:01.510 --> 01:51:06.530
The answer's probably like 10 to 15 to maybe
20 percent, max.

01:51:06.530 --> 01:51:10.050
So where did that other 80 percent come from?

01:51:10.050 --> 01:51:15.369
I'm arguing that if you want to participate
in Medicine 3.0, you have to be able to learn

01:51:15.369 --> 01:51:17.820
outside of your training.

01:51:17.820 --> 01:51:25.139
I think that's a discussion that should be
had very deliberately and not obliquely.

01:51:25.139 --> 01:51:29.409
So I think understanding where they sit on
prevention, understand how they're educating

01:51:29.409 --> 01:51:34.139
themselves, what are they reading, what are
they learning?

01:51:34.139 --> 01:51:36.800
If the answer is, "Hey, it's not a good fit,
great.

01:51:36.800 --> 01:51:38.820
Better to find that out before you jump in."

01:51:38.820 --> 01:51:40.510
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:51:40.510 --> 01:51:45.239
As we're talking about this, two things came
to mind, and please feel free to dismantle

01:51:45.239 --> 01:51:52.270
this, but the first is to be able to assess
medical literacy, you have to have a certain

01:51:52.270 --> 01:51:55.000
base level of medical literacy yourself.

01:51:55.000 --> 01:52:02.139
So I would say kind of step number one is
developing a working vocabulary and also frameworks,

01:52:02.139 --> 01:52:08.679
many of which you provide in Outlive, such
that you can actually assess other people

01:52:08.679 --> 01:52:10.210
to some extent.

01:52:10.210 --> 01:52:11.239
That's kind of step one.

01:52:11.239 --> 01:52:19.540
The second is that if we think about many
Western-trained, or I shouldn't say Western-trained.

01:52:19.540 --> 01:52:28.079
Physicians who are sort of schooled in a Western
paradigm as those focused on the sort of addressing

01:52:28.079 --> 01:52:31.469
of disease rather than prevention of disease,
broadly speaking.

01:52:31.469 --> 01:52:35.980
May be unfair to paint so broadly, but let's
stick with that for a minute, that as you

01:52:35.980 --> 01:52:41.750
think of your healthcare, it goes beyond just
your primary care physician and could very

01:52:41.750 --> 01:52:50.219
well and probably should include people like
exercise physiologists or people who are trainers,

01:52:50.219 --> 01:52:56.710
given how much overlap and integration we've
discussed in the course of our conversation.

01:52:56.710 --> 01:53:03.510
It just occurred to me that — this I think
is quite true, that it's a lot easier.

01:53:03.510 --> 01:53:07.500
Not that you shouldn't look for concierge
doctors if you have the means and that's a

01:53:07.500 --> 01:53:09.699
possibility, by all means do that.

01:53:09.699 --> 01:53:18.349
But you can find excellent, excellent strength
coaches, I would say, much more easily than

01:53:18.349 --> 01:53:24.099
you may be able to get yourself into a economic
position where you can afford concierge medicine

01:53:24.099 --> 01:53:25.230
and the payoffs.

01:53:25.230 --> 01:53:29.489
I'm not saying they're comparable, but the
payoffs of having a good strength coach or

01:53:29.489 --> 01:53:33.230
an endurance coach go a long way.

01:53:33.230 --> 01:53:37.020
Those came to mind also.

01:53:37.020 --> 01:53:39.590
Let's just say they're asking these questions
of their doctors.

01:53:39.590 --> 01:53:41.139
They find a doctor.

01:53:41.139 --> 01:53:47.449
Are there any particular recommendations you
would have?

01:53:47.449 --> 01:53:54.460
A couple of things come to mind that I have
in my notes here related to tests to request.

01:53:54.460 --> 01:53:59.790
For instance, I don't know where you stand
now on GRAIL, which I guess would be considered

01:53:59.790 --> 01:54:00.820
a liquid biopsy, right?

01:54:00.820 --> 01:54:02.050
Dr. Peter Attia: Yep.

01:54:02.050 --> 01:54:08.900
Tim Ferriss: Scans like Prenuvo for instance,
and perhaps more intelligent approach to family

01:54:08.900 --> 01:54:11.940
history is something that I know you cover
in the book.

01:54:11.940 --> 01:54:18.920
Would you like to speak to anything that might
fall in that category of things to consider

01:54:18.920 --> 01:54:19.920
requesting?

01:54:19.920 --> 01:54:25.340
Dr. Peter Attia: Yeah, we think it's really
important to be testing, as I said, APOE is

01:54:25.340 --> 01:54:26.340
one thing for sure.

01:54:26.340 --> 01:54:28.580
We really do want to know that genotype.

01:54:28.580 --> 01:54:37.099
And again, if you believe that Alzheimer's
disease has some element of prevention baked

01:54:37.099 --> 01:54:42.130
into it, then knowing that you're high risk
should be a valuable thing to understand.

01:54:42.130 --> 01:54:48.450
When it comes to understanding cardiovascular
disease, we care deeply about apoB.

01:54:48.450 --> 01:54:53.760
apoB, again being a more important metric
than LDL cholesterol, or non HDL cholesterol,

01:54:53.760 --> 01:54:55.260
or HDL cholesterol.

01:54:55.260 --> 01:55:00.420
This apoB is the concentration of all atherogenic
particles, so includes all the HDLs, VLDLs,

01:55:00.420 --> 01:55:01.420
et cetera.

01:55:01.420 --> 01:55:07.800
Also, knowing LPa, which is a genetically
determined very high-risk particle that while

01:55:07.800 --> 01:55:14.440
it can't be modified, will allow you to understand
how much more you need to optimize, for example,

01:55:14.440 --> 01:55:15.440
apoB.

01:55:15.440 --> 01:55:18.040
So knowing LPa, knowing apoB, knowing APOE.

01:55:18.040 --> 01:55:24.280
I think there are other biomarkers obviously,
that we care deeply about uric acid, homocysteine

01:55:24.280 --> 01:55:26.639
insulin, liver function tests.

01:55:26.639 --> 01:55:29.909
Cystatin C is one that I think is largely
underappreciated.

01:55:29.909 --> 01:55:35.710
So most doctors are using creatinine as the
measure of kidney function.

01:55:35.710 --> 01:55:37.040
Creatinine is a joke, actually.

01:55:37.040 --> 01:55:40.329
I, actually, at some point, I wish I had the
time, and maybe I'll make the time to do a

01:55:40.329 --> 01:55:44.869
deep dive into how it became the defacto gold
standard for measuring and estimating glomerular

01:55:44.869 --> 01:55:46.199
filtration rate.

01:55:46.199 --> 01:55:48.409
It's an absolute atrocity.

01:55:48.409 --> 01:55:53.940
For a person who's very low in muscle mass,
it really overestimates kidney function.

01:55:53.940 --> 01:55:59.429
For a person with high muscle mass or who's
just exercised, it really underestimates kidney

01:55:59.429 --> 01:56:03.020
function, but it virtually never correctly
estimates kidney function.

01:56:03.020 --> 01:56:06.811
Whereas Cystatin C, which is another blood
test, probably costs a few more dollars, but

01:56:06.811 --> 01:56:07.811
certainly not much.

01:56:07.811 --> 01:56:14.949
It might be the difference of $1 versus $7
or something, completely more accurate and

01:56:14.949 --> 01:56:18.530
unlinked to these issues of muscle mass.

01:56:18.530 --> 01:56:21.270
Tim Ferriss: How do you spell Cystatin C?

01:56:21.270 --> 01:56:27.700
Dr. Peter Attia: C-Y-S-T-A, Cystat, as it
sounds phonetically, I couldn't do the spelling

01:56:27.700 --> 01:56:28.700
bee.

01:56:28.700 --> 01:56:29.700
But it's C-Y.

01:56:29.700 --> 01:56:31.780
Tim Ferriss: People will find it on Google.

01:56:31.780 --> 01:56:33.219
Dr. Peter Attia: C-Y-S.

01:56:33.219 --> 01:56:34.219
Yeah, exactly.

01:56:34.219 --> 01:56:36.780
Don't ask me to spell anything longer than
"ah."

01:56:36.780 --> 01:56:38.219
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:56:38.219 --> 01:56:40.570
How about on the early cancer screening side?

01:56:40.570 --> 01:56:44.170
Dr. Peter Attia: Well, again, I think that
cancer chapter is probably a very long chapter

01:56:44.170 --> 01:56:51.469
for a reason, but I think one of the big takeaways
on that chapter is the overwhelming evidence

01:56:51.469 --> 01:56:57.680
that treating cancers when they are detected
early has significantly better outcomes than

01:56:57.680 --> 01:57:02.199
treating the exact same cancer later on down
the line, even using the same cocktail of

01:57:02.199 --> 01:57:03.199
drugs.

01:57:03.199 --> 01:57:06.869
I think the two examples in the book I use
are that of colon cancer and breast cancer,

01:57:06.869 --> 01:57:12.679
and looking at, for example, using the FOLFOX
regimen of chemotherapy to treat a patient

01:57:12.679 --> 01:57:17.670
with adjuvant treatment, meaning, so a person
that had a stage two or stage three colon

01:57:17.670 --> 01:57:23.369
cancer that is resected and at least surgically,
you don't see any gross disease, they just

01:57:23.369 --> 01:57:25.010
have microscopic disease.

01:57:25.010 --> 01:57:30.040
You treat those people with the FOLFOX chemo
regimen, which is a multi-drug regimen, and

01:57:30.040 --> 01:57:35.929
you compare that to people who have metastatic
disease, the outcomes are vastly different.

01:57:35.929 --> 01:57:41.590
In the latter group, nobody's going to survive
for 10 years and very few of them will make

01:57:41.590 --> 01:57:42.590
it five years.

01:57:42.590 --> 01:57:47.480
In the former group, 80 plus percent are going
to survive 10 years.

01:57:47.480 --> 01:57:49.030
Go through the similar example of breast cancer.

01:57:49.030 --> 01:57:55.199
The point here is the fewer tumor cells you
have, the fewer, the smaller the burden of

01:57:55.199 --> 01:58:00.600
cancer, the fewer the mutations, the fewer
the escape mechanisms that are in place to

01:58:00.600 --> 01:58:05.179
evade the immune system, the more likely you
are to survive treatment.

01:58:05.179 --> 01:58:10.910
And therefore, until we figure out ways to
completely eliminate cancer, which I don't

01:58:10.910 --> 01:58:15.600
think are going to happen anytime soon, we
have to take a much more aggressive posture

01:58:15.600 --> 01:58:20.550
towards screening and acknowledge, by the
way, the downside of that.

01:58:20.550 --> 01:58:26.429
There's a significant downside to more aggressive
screening, but if you can come to grips with

01:58:26.429 --> 01:58:31.889
that, you have a better chance of beating
cancer by far.

01:58:31.889 --> 01:58:38.239
And to your question, now, GRAIL becomes one
of the tools we would think about doing that.

01:58:38.239 --> 01:58:45.440
GRAIL is a company, I guess it's currently
or now probably owned by Illumina, and it

01:58:45.440 --> 01:58:48.160
uses something called cell-free DNA.

01:58:48.160 --> 01:58:55.670
It takes a blood, couple tubes of blood and
looks in there for tiny, tiny, tiny amounts

01:58:55.670 --> 01:58:57.840
of DNA that are out of the cell.

01:58:57.840 --> 01:59:02.520
Most of the DNA, Tim, if you went and did
a DNA test and they took blood, they would

01:59:02.520 --> 01:59:06.340
take the DNA out of cells in your blood, they'd
break down red blood cells and white blood

01:59:06.340 --> 01:59:07.719
cells and take the DNA.

01:59:07.719 --> 01:59:12.590
But this technique looks at all the DNA which
is not in cells, which is a fraction of a

01:59:12.590 --> 01:59:14.820
fraction of a fraction of a percent.

01:59:14.820 --> 01:59:20.429
They look at that DNA and by looking at the
methylation pattern of it, so these little

01:59:20.429 --> 01:59:24.869
methyl groups that are stuck to it, they impute
two things.

01:59:24.869 --> 01:59:28.719
One, is cancer likely to be present, yes or
no?

01:59:28.719 --> 01:59:33.000
And if yes, can we figure out what organ it
is?

01:59:33.000 --> 01:59:35.869
Where did it come from?

01:59:35.869 --> 01:59:45.530
These tests have a relatively low sensitivity,
meaning if a cancer is present, their ability

01:59:45.530 --> 01:59:48.349
to detect it is not that high.

01:59:48.349 --> 01:59:57.199
They have a very high specificity, which means
if no cancer is present, they're very likely

01:59:57.199 --> 01:59:58.409
to tell you that.

01:59:58.409 --> 02:00:02.969
Every screening test has to be tuned towards
sensitivity versus specificity.

02:00:02.969 --> 02:00:08.770
And this test is tuned to very high specificity,
very low sensitivity.

02:00:08.770 --> 02:00:14.780
Knowing the sensitivity and specificity of
a diagnostic test is only slightly useful.

02:00:14.780 --> 02:00:20.570
If you don't know the prevalence of the condition
you're testing for, which is called the pretest

02:00:20.570 --> 02:00:25.520
probability, you can't actually impute what's
called positive and negative predictive value.

02:00:25.520 --> 02:00:28.949
In other words, what you really want to know
when you look at a diagnostic test before

02:00:28.949 --> 02:00:33.550
you take it is if it's positive, how likely
is it that I have the condition?

02:00:33.550 --> 02:00:36.550
If it's negative, how likely is it that I
don't have the condition?

02:00:36.550 --> 02:00:39.260
That's positive and negative predictive value
respectively.

02:00:39.260 --> 02:00:42.739
And you can only answer that question when
not only the sensitivity and specificity,

02:00:42.739 --> 02:00:44.980
but what your pretest probability is.

02:00:44.980 --> 02:00:48.260
And if you don't know that, you would just
say, "Well, what's the prevalence of the condition

02:00:48.260 --> 02:00:49.929
in where I'm looking?"

02:00:49.929 --> 02:00:55.659
When you take cancer screening in a general
population, the prevalence is very low.

02:00:55.659 --> 02:00:58.660
That'd be one percent, two percent.

02:00:58.660 --> 02:01:02.929
100 people that walk into for a random cancer
screening, you wouldn't expect more than one

02:01:02.929 --> 02:01:04.889
or two of them have cancer.

02:01:04.889 --> 02:01:09.969
With a low sensitivity and a high specificity
test you have, if you do the math, you'll

02:01:09.969 --> 02:01:14.909
see, very low positive predictive value and
very high negative predictive value.

02:01:14.909 --> 02:01:21.639
And for that reason, I think initially was
not blown away by the test because of how

02:01:21.639 --> 02:01:24.860
low the sensitivity was.

02:01:24.860 --> 02:01:30.270
For low stage cancers, like stage one and
stage two, the sensitivity was like 20 percent.

02:01:30.270 --> 02:01:32.469
And that's the metric that I cared most about.

02:01:32.469 --> 02:01:36.300
I don't really care about your sensitivity
at detecting stage four cancer.

02:01:36.300 --> 02:01:40.179
We've blown it if we're waiting until that
point.

02:01:40.179 --> 02:01:45.690
What is very interesting to me though, is
when you start to look at this by histology,

02:01:45.690 --> 02:01:47.760
and this to me is interesting.

02:01:47.760 --> 02:01:54.280
If you look at all breast cancer, the stage
one, stage two sensitivity for detection is

02:01:54.280 --> 02:01:56.739
20-ish percent.

02:01:56.739 --> 02:02:03.570
But if you look at hormone negative breast
cancer, it's 75 percent.

02:02:03.570 --> 02:02:07.300
If you look at hormone positive cancer, it's
20 percent, basically.

02:02:07.300 --> 02:02:10.190
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by hormone negative
and positive?

02:02:10.190 --> 02:02:14.369
Dr. Peter Attia: One of the most important
ways that we identify breast cancer is by

02:02:14.369 --> 02:02:16.099
its hormone profile.

02:02:16.099 --> 02:02:21.100
Is it estrogen, progesterone, and/or HER2
neu positive?

02:02:21.100 --> 02:02:23.969
Triple negative breast cancer has the worst
prognosis.

02:02:23.969 --> 02:02:29.929
A breast cancer that does not express the
progesterone estrogen or HER2 neu receptors

02:02:29.929 --> 02:02:31.389
has the worst outcome.

02:02:31.389 --> 02:02:33.810
It's the most aggressive breast cancer.

02:02:33.810 --> 02:02:39.329
And those cancers even at the same stage are
far more detectable.

02:02:39.329 --> 02:02:45.710
They have a 75 percent sensitivity in low
stage cancer.

02:02:45.710 --> 02:02:51.280
This tells us that we need to think of liquid
biopsies in a very different way from how

02:02:51.280 --> 02:02:53.370
we think about "anatomic screening."

02:02:53.370 --> 02:03:03.320
A mammogram or an MRI or an ultrasound are
agnostic to the properties of the cancer.

02:03:03.320 --> 02:03:07.119
They are simply looking at its presence and
size.

02:03:07.119 --> 02:03:13.790
What this liquid biopsy might be giving us
a window into is the behavior of a cancer.

02:03:13.790 --> 02:03:17.130
Why is it that a triple negative, I have a
75 percent chance of — I'm using the terminology

02:03:17.130 --> 02:03:21.260
looser, but let's just say I got a 75 percent
chance of catching it in the bloodstream even

02:03:21.260 --> 02:03:22.820
at an early stage.

02:03:22.820 --> 02:03:26.400
Whereas if it's triple positive, which has
a much better outcome, I'm not going to catch

02:03:26.400 --> 02:03:28.580
it very likely at that stage.

02:03:28.580 --> 02:03:33.750
Might it be that because the latter is not
